400:
** ἀναφων-έω**,
**call aloud, shout,** {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.33.4, LXX.1Ch.15.28, +others, NT.Luke.1.42}}; **exercise the voice,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.3}}: especially **practise the voice** by declaiming, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.130c}}; τὰ πρός τι ἀναπεφωνημένα **declamations** upon.., {{Refs|[prev. work]30e.}}
**2** **proclaim,** βασιλέα{{Refs|[prev. author] “Demetr.” 18}}: with _dative_, **decree,** τοῦ ἀναπεφωνημένου Νουμηνίῳ στεφάνου{{Refs|“PFay.” 14.2.}}
**3** ἀ. τὴν ἐλευθερίαν **claim** liberty, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.58, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 27.}}
**4** of poetic **utterance,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 400a18, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 32}}; of any **utterance,** with _accusative_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.24U., compare p.27 U.}}
**5** **invoke,** in _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 125 (quater).}}

401:
** ἀνάχῠσις**, εως, ἡ, ἀναχέω
**expansion, effusion,** χολῆς{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.15; ἰκτεριώδης 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.48, compare 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “fragment” 79.9; πύου 3rd c.BC: Erasistratus Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.318; τῶν εἰδῶν εἰς τὸ ὄν 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 503.32: _metaphorically_, ἀ. ψυχῆς}}**exhilaration,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.187.}}
**b** **exhalation,** τοῦ ὠκεανοῦ (causing fogs) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|5th c.AD: Priscus Historicus p.341 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**2** ἡ τῆς ἀσωτίας ἀ. **excess** of profligacy, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.1Pet.4.4; τοῦ ἀλόγου πάθους 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.695}}; of enthusiasm, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 831.13.}}
**II** **expanse** of water, e.g. **estuary,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.1.9: _plural_, ἀ. θαλάττης}}**inundations,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Maximus Tyrius Sophista 38.3; ἀνάχυσιν λαμβανούσης τῆς θαλάσσης 1st c.BC(?): Ocellus Lucanus Philosophus 3.4.}}
**III** an expectorant, ={σύμφυτον}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.9.}}

402:
** ἀναχωρ-έω**,
_Locrian dialect_, Cretan dialect ἀνχ-, **go back,** πόλινδε ἂψ ἀναχωρήσουσιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.210, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.461.}}
**b** **walk backwards,** of oxen feeding, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.183.}}
**2** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”}}, mostly, **retire, withdraw** from battle, ἀλλά σ᾽ ἔγωγ᾽ ἀναχωρήσαντα κελεύω ἐς πληθὺν ἰέναι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.30; τόφρ᾽ ἀναχωρείτω 11.189, compare 4.305, 20.335, etc.: in Prose, μάχης οὔσης εἰς τοὐπίσω ἀ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.6; φυγῇ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 221a}}; generally, **retire, withdraw,** μεγάροιο μυχόνδε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.270; ὀπίσω ἀ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.94, etc.; ἐς τοὔπισθεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1208}}; ἀνεκεχωρήκεσαν **they had retired** or **returned,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.15, compare “IG” 9(1).334 (_Locrian dialect_)}}: with Preps. denoting motion to or from, ἐς τὴν ἀκρόπολιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.143; ἐπ᾽ οἴκου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.30}}; ὑπὸ Βοιωτῶν ἐς Ἀθήνας **were forced** by them **to retire to..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.61; ἀπό 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Smp.” [prev. cited]}}
**II** **come back** or **revert to the rightful heir,** ἡ βασιληΐη ἀνεχώρησε ἐς τὸν παῖδα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.4; ἡ ποινὴ ἀ. εἰς ἡμᾶς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.3, compare “Leg.Gort.” 11.10.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **withdraw, retire,** ἐξ αἰσθήσεων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 83a}}; ἀ. ἐκ τῶν πραγμάτων **retire from** public life, **from** the world, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 29.25.5, compare 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 9.4.2, NT.Matt.2.14, +others}}: _absolutely_, **withdraw, retire,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 524}}; ἀνακεχωρηκυῖα χώρα **inland** spot, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.7.4; ἀ. ἀπὸ θαλάσσης 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.11.16; ἀνακεχωρηκός ῥῆμα, ὄνομα}}**obsolete,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 10.7}}; **recondite,** ἱστορία{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.157S.}}
**IV** ={συγχωρέω}, πάντες ἀνεχώρησαν συμπεραίνεσθαι τὸ μίασμα {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 10.}}
**IV.2** **strike, refuse to work,** {{Refs|PTeb. 26.18, 41.4, +others; ἀνακεχωρηκότα σώματα [prev. work]5.6.}}

403:
** ἀνά-ψυξις**, εως, ἡ,
**cooling,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 72}}; **exposure,** ἑλκεος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 25.}}
**2** **drying up,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.2.19.}}
**3** **relief, respite,** {{LXX+4th c.AD+|LXX.Exo.8.15; κακῶν 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator}}**ad Them.**258c; καιροὶ -εως{{NT|NT.Act.3.20.}}

404:
** ἀνα-ψύχω** [ῡ],
_Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ **-εσκον**{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 562}}:—**cool, refresh,** ἀήτας Ὠκεανὸς ἀνίησιν ἀναψύχειν ἀνθρώπους{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.568}}; ἀνέψυχον φίλον ἦτορ **were reviving** their spirit, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.84; ἕλκος ἀναψύχοντα 5.795; ἀ. φίλα γούνατα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 608}}; ἀ. βάτιν **cool** the feet **in water,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 421}}:—_passive_, **to be revived, refreshed,** ἀνέψυχθεν φίλον ἦτορ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.575}}; of the body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 78e, compare 70d}}; ὥστ᾽ ἀνεψύχης [ῠ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Amipsias Comicus 13.}}
**2** ναῦς ἀ. **let** the ships **rest and get dry,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.59, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.10}}; ἀ. τὸν ἱδρῶτα **let** it **dry off,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 29}}; ἀ. τὰς αὐλαίας **dry** them, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Them.” 30}}:—_passive_, **to be dried up,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.2.19.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, with _genitive_, ἀ. πόνων τινά **give** him **relief from** toil, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1094; πολέων.. μεριμνέων 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hecale” 1.1.7; ἀ. κακότητος ψυχάς 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 230: _absolutely_, NT.2Tim.1.16.}}
**4** in Medicine texts, **expose** to air, κατὰ τὸ ἕλκος ἐῶσιν ἀνεψῦχθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 25}}; compare ἀναψύχειν· γυμνοῦν, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus}}
**II** the _active_ is also used _intransitive_, **become cool, recover, revive,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 81, “Anthologia Graeca” 12.132 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 5.623}}; εὗρεν.. δρόμων ἀναψύχουσαν [τὴν ἔλαφον] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 95.57}}; **take relaxation,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 1296.7 (3rd c.AD).}}

407:
** ἀνδρίζω**,
**make a man of, make manly,** τοὺς γεωργοῦντας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 5.4.}}
**II** mostly in _passive_ or _middle_, **come to manhood,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 744, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 228, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 15.}}
**II.2** **play the man,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.34, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 151d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1115b4, LXX.Josh.1.6, +others, NT.1Cor.16.13}}; **dress like a man,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.2.}}
**II.3** obscene meaning, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 79.5}}; of a eunuch, ἀ. ἐπὶ γυναῖκα{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.37, compare “Ep.” 54, 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 4.1.}}

409:
** ἀνδρο-φόνος**, ον,
**man-slaying,** Homeric epithet of Hector, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.724, etc.}}; of Achilles, χεῖρες ἀ.{{Refs|18.317}}; **homicide,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 114a}}; generally, **murderous,** ἀ. τὴν φύσιν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 217}}:—rarely except of **slaughter in battle,** but in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.261 φάρμακον ἀ.}} a **murderous** drug:—epithet of αῖμα, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Hymni” 65.4.}}
**2** of women, **murdering their husbands,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.252.}}
**II** as law-term, **one convicted of manslaughter, homicide,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.7, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.29, compare [prev. work]216:—hence as a term of abuse, τοὺς ἀ. ἰχθυοπώλας 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 6.228c, compare 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 30.}}
**III** ἀ. Κῶνος, a landmark at Athens, {{Refs|“IG” 3.61 A ii 15.}}

410:
** ἀνέγκλη-τος**, ον,
**without reproach, blameless,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.13, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 2.14; διαφυλάττειν τοὺς πολίτας ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1360a16; ἀ. ἑαυτὸν παρέχειν “IG” 22.1271, compare “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2270.7 (from Delos)}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 17.2, “SIG” 436. 6 (Delph., 3rd c.BC), “PIand.” 33.14 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **giving no ground for dispute,** ἀ. τὰς οὐσίας πρὸς ἀλλήλους κατασκευάζεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 737a}}. _adverb_ -τως, ἔχειν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1321b22.}}
**III** _active_ in _adverb_ **-τως** **uncomplainingly,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.102e.}}

412:
** ἀνεκ-λάλητος**, ον,
**unutterable, ineffable,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.1Pet.1.8, 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Vitae Sophistarum” p.486 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus Byzantine “Epit.” 26.10, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 5.158d.}}
**2** **not capable of expression** or **calculation,** δύναμις{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus}}**Eup.Praef.;** ἰδιότης{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in Scholia 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 45.2.}}

413:
** ἀνέκ-λειπτος**, ον,
**incessant, uninterrupted,** Hy.p.{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Epit.” 20 (uncertain), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.84, “PLond.” 3.1166.7 (1st c.AD)}}; **infinite,** of divisions of space, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 10.141}}; **unfailing,** {{LXX+NT+1st c.BC+|LXX.Wis.7.14, NT.Luke.12.33, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.36, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 84}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.50, Heroap. 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 4.75.}}
**2** ἀ. μᾶζα, in Alchemy, of the asem alloy, {{Refs|“PHolm.” 2.17, “PLeid.X.” 7 and 39.}}

414:
** ἀνεκτός**, όν,
later ή, όν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“IGRom.” 4.293 ii 4 (Pergam.), 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.36}}: _Aeolic dialect_ ὄνεκτος {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 27.9}}:— **bearable, sufferable, tolerable,** mostly with a _negative_ (like ἀνασχετός), λοίγια ἔργα.. οὐδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἀνεκτά{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.573; χρειὼ.. οὐκέτ᾽ ἀνεκτός 10.118, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1195, etc.}}: so mostly in _Attic dialect_, οὐκ ἀνεκτόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1364; οὐκ ἀνεκτά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 282, etc.}}; or with a question, ἦ ταῦτα δῆτ᾽ ἀνεκτ;{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 429; ταυτὶ δῆτ᾽ ἀνέκτ᾽ ἀκούει; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 563:}} —οὐκ ἀνεκτόν [ἐστι] followed by _infinitive_, with or without μὴ οὐ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 154c, 181b}}; τὸ μὲν οὐκ ἀ. ἐμοὶ.. γίγνεσθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 861d.}}
**2** without a _negative_, τὸ μὲν καὶ ἀνεκτὸν ἔχει κακόν **that can be endured,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.83; ἀ. χοὖτος ἦν ὅμως ἐμοί 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 145.13; ἀνεκτὰ παθεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.77; μέχρι τοῦδε ἀνεκτοὶ οἱ ἔπαινοι ἐς ὅσον.. [prev. author] 2.35}}; παντὶ τρόπῳ ὅστις καὶ ὁπωσοῦν ἀνεκτός in any **tolerable** manner whatsoever, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.90; ἀ. τι λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.65; συμβίωσιν -όν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.78W.; ἀνεκτότερα}}**more tolerable,** {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 12.45.2; ἀνεκτότερον ἔσται τινί NT.Matt.10.15, 11.22}}, etc.: _superlative_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.226S.}}
**2.b** of persons, μόγις ἀνεκτοί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.20, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.13.}}
**II** _adverb_ -τῶς, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus always οὐκέτ᾽ ἀνεκτῶς, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.350, etc.}}; οὐκ ἀνεκτῶς ἔχει it is not **to be borne,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.3.1: without _negative_, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3 “fragment” 2, “Oec.” p.31J.}}

417:
** ἄνεμος** [ᾰ], ὁ,
**wind,** πέτετο πνοιῇ ἀνέμοιο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.207; ἀνέμων ἀτάλαντοι ἀέλλῃ 13.795; ὦρσεν.. ἀνέμοιο θύελλαν 12.253; ἀνέμοιο.. δεινὸς ἀήτης 15.626, compare 14.254; ἀνέμων ἀμέγαρτον ἀϋτμήν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.407, etc.; ἀνέμων πνεύματα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16.ά, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 102; ῥιπαί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 137.930 (both Lyric poetry); ἀήματα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 905; αὖραι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 838; πνοιαί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1396; ἀνέμων φθόγγος 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 37.10}}; ἀνέμου κατιόντος μεγάλου **a gale** having come on, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.25}}; ἀνέμου ἐξαίφνης ἀσελγοῦς γενομένου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 320}}; ἄνεμος κατὰ βορέαν ἑστηκώς **the wind** being set in the north, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.104}}; ἀνέμοις φέρεσθαι παραδιδόναι τι cast a thing **to the winds,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 419, compare 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1334}}; κατ᾽ ἄνεμον στῆναι stand **to leeward,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 541a26, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.972a; κατ᾽ ἄνεμον καὶ ῥοῦν νήχεσθαι [prev. work]979c: _metaphorically_, ἄνεμος.. ἄνθρωπος}} 'unstable as the wind', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 376}}; φέρειν τιν᾽ ἄρας (sic l.) ἄ. **a very wind** to carry off, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 195.5 (from Lobeck)}}; ἀνέμους θηρᾶν ἐν δικτύοις try to catch **the wind,** and ἀνέμῳ διαλέγεσθαι talk **to the wind,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Zenobius Paroemiographus 1.38; ἀνέμους γεωργεῖν 'plough the sands', [prev. work] 100.}}
**2** **cardinal point, quarter,** ἐκ τῶν τεσσάρων ἀ.{{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Zech.2.6, “Annales du Service” 19.40 (Theadelphia, 1st c.BC), NT.Matt.24.31, +others, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 140.6, “PFlor.” 50.104: _sing._, [prev. work] 20.19 (2nd c.AD)}}; τὸ κατ᾽ ἄνεμον **aspect,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 100.10 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **wind** in the body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.179, +others (From ἀνε-}} 'blow, breathe', cf. Sanskrit _áni-ti_ 'breathes', Gothic _uzanan_ 'expire', etc.)

424:
** ἀνέρχομαι**,
(compare ἄνειμι): _aorist_ -ήλυθον or -ῆλθον:— **go up,** ἀνελθὼν ἐς σκοπιήν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.97; εἰς τὴν ἀκρόπολιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.39; ἐπὶ τὴν σκηνήν 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.22.26; ἐπὶ βῆμα 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.5.2}}: _absolutely_, **mount the tribune,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 31}}; **go up from** the coast inland, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.190}}; **come up from** the nether world, ἀ. ἐξ Ἀΐδεω{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 703; κἀξ Ἅιδου θανὼν πρὸς φῶς ἀ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 625; ἐξ Ἅιδου εἰς θεούς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 521c.}}
**2** of trees, **grow up, shoot up,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.163, 167}}; of the sun, **rise,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 658; ἀ. ὠκεανοῖο 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1230}}; of water, **rise,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 358b32, 4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 1.23: _metaphorically_, ὄλβος ἀ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 810.}}
**3** **go up to** a first principle, in argument, ἐπ᾽ ἀρχὴν ἀνελθόντες σκοπεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 511d.}}
**II** **go or come back, return,** ἂψ or αὖθις ἀ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|11.4.392, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.317.}}
**II.2** **come back to** a point, **recur to** it **and say,** ἄνελθέ μοι πάλιν τί..{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1207, compare “Ion” 933}}; πάλιν ἐπ᾽ ἀρχὴν ἀ. variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 69a.}}
**II.3** νόμος.. εἴς σ᾽ ἀνελθὼν εἰ διαφθαρήσεται **being brought home to** you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 802. [In 11.4.392, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.821, ᾱνερχομένῳ is corrupt.]}}
**III** _transitive_, **traverse,** νειόν{{Refs|Callias Epigrammaticus “Aet.fragment” 7.4P.}}

425:
** ἄνεσις**,
_genitive_ εως, _Ionic dialect_ ιος, ἡ: (ἀνιημι):— **loosening, relaxing,** τῶν χορδῶν of the strings, opposed to ἐπίτασις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 349e}}; coupled with χάλασις,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 590b; τῆς αἰσθήσεως.. δεσμὸν τὸν ὕπνον εἶναί φαμεν, τὴν δέ.. ἄνεσιν ἐγρήγορσιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Somno et Vigilia” 454b27; ἀέρος 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.1.6}}; πάγων ἄ., i.e. a **thaw,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sertorius” 17}}; of the **ebb**-tide, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 7.2.1.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **remission, abatement,** κακῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.28}}; opposed to θλῖψις, {{NT|NT.2Cor.8.13, +others}}; λύπης, μοχθηρίας, etc., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.102b, etc.; τὴν ἡδονὴν ἄνεσιν λαμβάνειν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3 “fragment” 1}}; ἄ. φόρων, τελῶν, **remission** of tribute, taxes, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sertorius” 6, “IG” 7.2227 (from Thisbe), etc.; κολάσεως 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.3.24}}; of fevers, opposed to παροξυσμός, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.427.}}
**3** **relaxation, recreation,** opposed to σπουδή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 724a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1371b34, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Cleanthes Stoicus 1.122; ἄ. καὶ σχολή 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.66.10; ψυχῆς 3rd c.BC: Mnesitheus Atheniensis Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.484a.}}
**4** **solution,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.96.}}
**5** ={τὸ τελευταῖον τῆς παρακμῆς} {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.424.}}
**II** **indulgence, licence,** ἡδονῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 561a; ἡ τῶν γυναικῶν παρ᾽ ὑμῖν ἄ. [prev. author] “Lg.” 637c, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1270a1; δούλων [prev. work] 1313b35}}; **relaxation of custody,** {{NT|NT.Acts.24.23.}}
**III** of musical pitch, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus 1.5}}; of an unaccented syllable, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 2.18.}}

426:
** ἀνετάζω**,
**inquire of,** ἀλλήλους τὴν αἰτίαν{{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Sus.14 (2nd c.AD(?): Theodotion LXX), LXX.Judg.6.29 (codex A).}}
**II** **examine** documents, {{Refs|“POxy.” 34i13}}; **examine by torture,** τινά{{NT|NT.Act.22.24, compare 29.}}

427:
** ἄνευ**,
Megarian and Hellenistic ἄνις (which see); ἄνευν{{Refs 6th c.AD+|“IG” 4.1484.58 (Epid.); ἄνευς “GDI” 1157 (6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus):}} —_preposition_ (never used in _composition_) with _genitive_ (with _accusative_ only {{Refs|“GDI” [same place])}}, **without,** opposed to σύν, ἄνευ ἕθεν οὐδὲ σὺν αὐτῷ 11.17.407; ἄ. κέντροιο **without** the goad, 23.387; μόνος ἄ. τινός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 143, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 217a}}; in pregnant sense, ἄ. θεῶν, mostly with _negative_, οὔτι ἄ. θεοῦ ἥδε γε βουλή{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.372; οὔ τοι ἄ. θεοῦ ἔπτατο.. ὄρνις 15.531; οὐκ ἄ. θεῶν τινος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 164; μηδὲ θύεσθαι μέν τιν᾽ ἰδίᾳ ἄ. τοῦ ἄρχοντος 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 10.4}}; also without _negative_, ἄ. ἐμέθεν **without** my knowledge and will, {{Refs|11.15.213}}; ἄ. πολιτᾶν **without** their consent, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 431; ἄ. τοῦ κραίνοντος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 926}}; ἄ. τοῦ ὑγιεινοῦ **without reference to** health, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 518d, compare 519a; οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ζῆν ἄ. κακοῦ τινος 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 32.12,etc.}}
**II** **away from, far from,** ἄ. δηΐων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.13.556; ἄ. ὄψου ποιεῖν τινας ἑστιωμένους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372c, compare “Hp.Ma.” 290e.}}
**III** in Prose, **except, besides,** πάντα ἄ. χρυσοῦ{{Refs|[prev. author] “Criti.” 112c; ἄ. τοῦ καλὴν δόξαν ἐνεγκεῖν}}**praeterquam quod attulerit..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.89}}; καὶ ἄ. τοῦ λαμβάνειν even **without** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.28.}}—In early writers it rarely follows its case, ὑφηγητοῦ δ᾽ ἄ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 502; ὧν ἄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.14}}; frequently in later Prose, as always in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, “Metaphorical” 1071a2, +others, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.47c, etc.}} (Cf. Gothic _inu,_ OHG. ᾱνο 'without'; perhaps akin to _negative_ pref. ἀ-.)

429:
** ἀνευρ-ίσκω**,
_future_ -ευρήσω: _aorist_ -εῦρον, later **-ευράμην**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1133:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ -ευρέθην:—**find out, discover,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.67, 2.54, etc.; ἀγαθὰ ἀ. λογιζόμενος [prev. author] 7.8. ; ἀ. φόνον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1094}}; σὸν χρέος ἀνευρίσκειν πότερον.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 883; ἀ. τὴν αἰτίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 100b; τὴν τοῦ θεοῦ φύσιν “Phdr.” 252e, etc.}}:—_middle_, **win, gain,** ἱερὸν χῶρον ἀνευρομένην{{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 259}}:—_passive_, **to be found out** or **discovered,** ὡς ὕστερον ἀνευρέθη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.128}}: with _participle_, ἀνεύρηται ὁμοῖα παρεχομένη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.44.}}
**II** **think out, invent,** μόνος ἀνευρηκὼς τέχνην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 113, compare 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 37, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 273c; ἀ. πρόφασίν τινα 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 88.10, etc.}}

430:
** ἀνέχω**,
_imperfect_ ἀνεῖχον: also ἀνίσχω, _imperfect_ ἀνῖσχον: _future_ ἀνέξω{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 82, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 4(s. see l.), also ἀνασχήσω 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.106, 7.14, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 732}}: _aorist_ ἀνέσχον{{Refs|11.17.310, etc.}}; _poetry_ ἀνέσχεθον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 10.461, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1027}}, _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ ἀνσχεθέειν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.320}}: _perfect_ ἀνέσχηκα{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.190, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 105}}:—_middle_ ἀνέχομαι: _imperfect_ ἠνειχόμην (with double augment) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 905, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 411, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77, etc.}}: _future_ ἀνέξομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.5.895, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1028, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.160, etc.; also ἀνασχήσομαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 252, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 299}}, _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ ἀνσχήσεσθαι{{Refs|11.5.104}}: _aorist_ ἀνεσχόμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|18.430, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 747 codices, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 687 (where ἠνέσχου is contra metrically)}}; more frequently with double augment ἠνεσχόμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.48, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1274}}; and _Attic dialect_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1363, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.28, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.3, etc.; syncopated. ἠνσχόμην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 467; _2nd pers.sing._ _imperative_ ἄνσχεο (see. below with 11)}}:—_passive_, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.55, LXX.4Mac.1.35.}}
**A** _transitive_, **hold up, lift up,** χεῖρας ἀνέσχον **held up** their hands **in fight** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.89}} (later of pugilists, **hold up** the hands **in token of defeat,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.129)}}:—frequently **lift up** the hands **in prayer,** θεοῖσι δὲ χεῖρας ἀνέσχον{{Refs 7th c.BC+|11.3.318, compare 1.450, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 82, etc.}}; so ἄνακτι εὐχὰς ἀ. **offer** prayers, perhaps **with uplifted hands,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 636; ἄνεχε χέρας, ἄνεχε λόγον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 592}}; also ἀ. τὴν χεῖρα **offer** the hand (to shake), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 82 (uncertain).}}
**A.2** **lift up** as an offering, τάγ᾽ Ἀθηναίῃ ληΐτιδι.. ὑψόσ᾽ ἀνέσχεθε χειρί{{Refs|11.10.461}}; as a testimony, σκῆπτρον ἀ. πᾶσι θεοῖσι{{Refs|7.412}}; μαζὸν ἀ., of Hecuba entreating her son Hector,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|22.80; κενεὰς.. ἀνέσχε γλήνας 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.254; ἄκουε δ᾽ ἀν᾽ οὖς ἔχων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 126.}}
**A.3** ἀ. φλόγα **hold up** a torch, especially at weddings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 732: hence the phrase ἄνεχε, πάρεχε i.e. τὸ φῶς)}} **hold up,** pass on the light in procession, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 308, “Cyc.” 203, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1326; also ἀ. φάος σωτήριον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 482; τὸ σημεῖον τοῦ πυρός 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.111.}}
**A.4** **lift up, exalt,** τὰ κείνων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.89.}}
**A.5** **hold up, prop, sustain,** οὐρανὸν καὶ γῆν, of Atlas, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.11.5; κίων ἀ. τὴν στέγην 2nd c.AD: Oenomaus Philosophus cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 5.34}}:—_passive_, γέφυρα σκάφαις ἀνεχομένη{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.55:}} —but more frequently,
**A.5.b** _metaphorically_, **uphold, maintain,** εὐδικίας{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.111; πολέμους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141}}; ὄργια ἀ. **keep up** the revels, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 948; Βάκχης ἀνέχων λέκτρ᾽ Ἀγαμέμνων}}**remaining constant to,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 121 (see. below 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3)}}; οἰνῶπ᾽ ἀνέχουσα κισσόν **keeping constant to, haunting** the ivy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 674 (assuming variant)}}; βαρὺν ἀνὰ θυμὸν ἔχοισα **keeping up** his anger, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.96.}}
**A.6** **put forth,** δάφνα ἀ. πτόρθους{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 459 (Lyric poetry).}}
**A.II** **hold back, check,** ἄνεχ᾽ ἵππους{{Refs 1st c.BC+|11.23.426; ἀ. τὰ ὅπλα διὰ τῶν ἀνακλητικῶν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.21}}; ἀ. Σικελίαν μὴ ὑπ᾽ αὐτοὺς εἶναι **keep** it from being.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.86; ἑαυτὸν ἀπό τινος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.514a}}:—_passive_, ἀνέχεται τὰ πάθη ὑπὸ τοῦ λογισμοῦ{{LXX|LXX.4Mac.1.35.}}
**B** _intransitive_, **rise up, emerge,** ἀνσχεθέειν.. ὑπὸ κύματος ὁρμῆς{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.320}}; of a diver, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.8; σκόπελοι ἐν τῷ Νείλῳ ὀξέες ἀ. [prev. author] 2.29; ἀ. ἐς ἀέρα 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1383.}}
**B.b** especially in form ἀνίσχω, of the sun, πρὸς ἥλιον ἀνίσχοντα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.98, etc.; so λαμπὰς ἀνίσχει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 93 (Lyric poetry); ἅμ᾽ ἡλίῳ ἀνίσχοντι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.13, compare 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 119.9.}}
**B.c** of events, **arise, happen,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.106, 7.14.}}
**B.d** **appear, show oncself,** ἄελπτον ὄμμα.. φήμης ἀνασχόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 204}}; **turn out, prove to be,** μελοποιὸς ἢ τραγῳδὸς ἄριστος{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.209D.}}
**B.e** **stand up,** κίονες περὶ τοίχοις{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.217.}}
**B.2** **come forth,** αἰχμὴ παρὰ.. ὦμον ἀνέσχεν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|11.17.310, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 44}}; of a headland, **jut out** into the sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.123, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.46, etc.; ἀ. πρὸς τὸ Σικελικὸν πέλαγος [prev. author] 4.53, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.166}}; ἐς τὸν πόντον [τὴν ἄκρην] ἀνέχοντα **jutting out** with its headland into the sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.99 (uncertain reading); reversely, κοιλάδες ἐς μεσόγαιαν ἐκ θαλάσσης ἀ. 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.2.4.}}
**B.3** **hold on, keep doing,** with _participle_, ἀ. διασκοπῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.48}}; σε.. στέρξας ἀνέχει **is constant** in his love for thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 212 (Lyric poetry, compare above 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.5b): with _dative_, τελεταῖς}}**practise regularly,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.249 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}: _absolutely_, **wait,** ταύτῃ ἀνέχειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.94, compare 2.18.}}
**B.4** **hold up, cease,** Ζεὺς οὔθ᾽ ὕων πάντεσσ᾽ ἁνδάνει οὔτ᾽ ἀνέχων{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 26, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.28; uncertain reading in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.20.}}
**B.5** with _genitive_, **cease from,** οὐδὲ.. καμάτων ἀνέχουσι γυναῖκες{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 174; τοῦ πολέμου 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Αιβυκή” 75; τοῦ φονεύειν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 33.— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses no tense _intransitive_ except}}_aorist_
**C** _middle_, **hold up what is one's own,** ὁ δ᾽ ἀνέσχετο μείλινον ἔγχος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|11.5.655; δούρατ᾽ ἀνασχόμενοι 11.594, etc.: hence ἀνασχόμενος is often used _absolutely_ (i.e. ἔγχος, ξίφος, etc.), πλῆξεν ἀ. 3.362; κόψε δ᾽ ἀ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.425; πὺξ μάλ᾽ ἀνασχομένω πεπληγέμεν 11.23.660; also ἄντα δ᾽ ἀνασχομένω χερσί [prev. work] 686.}}
**C.II** **hold oneself up, bear up,** οὐδέ σ᾽ ὀΐω δηρὸν ἔτ᾽ ἀνσχήσεσθαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 5.285, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.375:}}_aorist_ _imperative_ ἀνάσχεο, ={τέτλαθι}, **be of good courage,**{{Refs|11.1.586; ἄνσχεο}}**be patient,** {{Refs|23.587}}; ἀνὰ δ᾽ ἔχευ is probable reading for ἀνὰ δ᾽ εὖ in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 6.2: in}}_present_ _participle_, ἀνεχόμενοι φέρουσι τὸν χειμῶνα they bear **with patience,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.28; Stoic motto ἀνέχου καὶ ἀπέχου 2nd c.AD: Gellius, Aulus Grammaticus Latinus 17.19.}}
**C.II.2** with _accusative_, τοσσάδ᾽ ἐνὶ φρεσὶν ᾗσιν ἀνέσχετο κήδεα 11.18.430; ἦ δὴ πολλὰ κάκ᾽ ἄνσχεο σὸν κατὰ θυμόν 24.518; τὴν δουλοσύνην οὐκ ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.169; τὰ πρὶν κακὰ ἠνειχόμεσθα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 905, etc.; χαλκὸν ἀνασχέσθαι 11.4.511, etc.: with _accusative_ _person_, οὐ γὰρ ξείνους.. ἀνέχονται}} they **do** not **suffer** or **bear with** strangers, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.32, compare 17.13; τῶν ἵππων οὔτι ἀνεχομένων τὰς καμήλους 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.87; τούτους ἀνάσχου δεσπότας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 304, compare 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 6 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}: with _accusative_ _of things_ et _genitive_ _person_, οὐδεὶς ἂν αὐτοῦ ἀγελαστὶ ἠνέσχετο ταῦτα τὰ ἔπη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.188c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 507.}}
**C.II.3** with _genitive_, uncertain in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, δουλοσύνης ἀνέχεσθαι variant in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.423; so ἅπαντος ἀνδρὸς ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 323a, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.16}}; **to be content with,** τοῦ ἐν σώματι κάλλους{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.9.2.}}
**C.II.4** the dependent clause is mostly (always in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus) in}}_participle_, οὐ μάν σε.. ἀνέξομαι ἄλγε᾽ ἔχοντα I **will** not **suffer** thee to have.., {{Refs|11.5.895}}; οὐ γὰρ ἀεργὸν [ὄντα] ἀνέξομαι I **will** not **suffer** one [to be].., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.27; εἰ τὸν.. θανόντ᾽ ἄθαπτον ἠνσχόμην νέκυν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 467; οὐκ ἀνέξεται τίκτοντας ἄλλους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 712}}; καὶ γάρ κ᾽.. ἀνεχοιμην ἥμενος for I **would** **be content** to sit.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.595; σοῦ κλύων ἀνέξεται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 838, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1028, “Ph.” 411; ἀνάσχεσθε σιγῶσαι [prev. author] “fragment” 679; also οὐ σῖγ᾽ ἀνέξε; [prev. author] “Aj.” 75: frequently in Prose, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80, 206, 5.19, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.74, etc.; ἄποτος ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 596a2; also ἀ. τοῦ ἄλλα λέγοντος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 564d; ἀ. τῶν οἰκείων ἀμελουμένων [prev. author] “Ap.” 31b; οὐδ᾽ ἂν ἠνέσχεσθε εἴ τις.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.170}}:—also in _active_, ἀνέσχηκα{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 105.}}
**C.II.5** rarely with _infinitive_, **suffer,** οὐκ ἀνέξομαι τὸ μὴ οὐ..{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 914; κοκκύζειν τὸν ἀλεκτρυόν᾽ οὐκ ἀνέχονται 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 311; ἀνακεκλίσθαι οὐκ ἀ. 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.9; ἀ. πάντα ὑπομένειν 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.34; σὺν ἄλλοις βιοῦν οὐκ ἀ. 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 6.30.}}
**C.II.5.b** **dare to** do, ἀνέσχοντο τὸν ἐπιόντα δέξασθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.139.}}
**C.II.5.c** οὐκ ἀ., with _infinitive_, **refuse** to do.., {{Refs|“POxy.” 903.36, +others}}
**C.III** rarely, **hold on by one another, hang together,** ἀνά τ᾽ ἀλλήλησιν ἔχονται{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.8.}}

433:
** ἀνήκω**,
**to have come up to** a point, **reach up to,** of persons and things, ἐς μέτρον τινὸς ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.127; αἱμασιὴν ὕψος ἀνήκουσαν ἀνδρὶ ἐς τὸν ὀμφαλόν [prev. author] 7.60; ἐς τὰ μέγιστα ἀ. ἀρετῆς πέρι 5.49; χρήμασι ἀ. ἐς τὰ πρῶτα 7.134}}; φρενῶν ἐς τὰ ἐμεωυτοῦ πρῶτα οὔκω ἀ. **have** not yet **reached** the highest point I aim at, {{Refs|[prev. work] 13; οὐκ ἐς τοσοῦτο εὐηθίης ἀ. [prev. work] 16.γ, compare 9.; πρόσω ἀρετῆς ἀ. [prev. work] 237}}; ἀ. εἰς τὸ ὀξύ **to rise to** a point, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 1.55}}; τοῦτο μὲν ἐς οὐδὲν ἀ. **amounts to** nothing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.104}}; μεῖζον ἀ. ἢ κατ᾽ ἐμὰν ῥώμαν the matter **has gone** too **far..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1018; αἱ πολλαὶ [ζημίαι].. ἐς τὸν θάνατον ἀ.}}**have gone as far as..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45.}}
**2** ἀ. ἐς σὲ ἔχειν it **has come to** you to have, **has become** yours to have, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109.}}
**3** ἀ. εἴς τι **refer to** or **be connected with**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.6, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1167b4 (variant)}}; τὰ εἰς ἀργυρίου λόγον ἀ. ἀδικήματα which **involve** a money consideration, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.60; so ὁ φόνος ἀνήκει εἴς τινα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.7; ἀ. πρός τι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.15.4, 3rd c.BC(?): Callixinus Historicus 2, etc.}}
**II** **belong, appertain,** {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.1Mac.10.42, +others; τὰ εἰς τιμὴν καὶ δόξαν ἀνήκοντα “OGI” 763.36 (Pergam.); τὰ ἐκείνοις -οντα [prev. work]532 (Paphlag.); τὰ ἀ. τῇπόλει “Inscription Magn.” 53.65 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὰ ἀ. τοῖς ἱεροῖς{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 6.42 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.2** _absolutely_, **to be fit** or **proper,** {{NT|NT.Eph.5.4, NT.Col.3.18}}; τὸ ἀνῆκον, ={τὸ προσῆκον}, {{NT|NT.Philm.8.}}
**III** **come back,** εἰς τοὺς πρώτους πάλιν ἀ. λόγους{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 196b.}}

435:
** ἀνήρ**, ὁ, ἀνδρός, ἀνδρί, ἄνδρα,
_vocative_ ἄνερ: _plural_ ἄνδρες, -δρῶν, -δράσι [ᾰ], -dras: _Aeolic dialect_ _dative_ _plural_ ἄνδρεσι{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 14.8: late _nominative_ _sing._ ἄνδρας “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 7.109.7: in _Attic dialect_ the _Article_ often forms a crasis with the Noun, ἁνήρ for ὁ ἀνήρ, τἀνδρός, τἀνδρί for τοῦ ἀνδρός, etc., ἅνδρες}} for οἱ ἄνδρε; the _Ionic dialect_ crasis is ὡνήρ, ὧνδρες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.161, 134: _Epic dialect_ also ἀνέρα, ἀνέρος, ἀνέρι, _dual_ ἀνέρε, _plural_ ἀνέρες, ἀνέρας, ἄνδρεσσι.}} [_Epic dialect_ Poets mostly use ᾱ in arsi, ᾰ in thesi; but in _trisyllable_ forms with stem ἀνέρ- always ; so also Trag. in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1011, “OT” 869. But in Trag. senarians ᾰ always.] (ἀ-}} in _nominative_ by analogy; cf. Sanskrit _nar-_ from I.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **ner-, nṛ-** from **nṛ-,** Gk. ἀνδρ- from **ṇr-**):—**man,** opposed to **woman** (ἄνθρωπος being **man** as opposed to to **beast**), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.435, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.323}}; τῶν ἀνδρῶν ἄπαις without **male** children, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 877e; in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} mostly of princes, leaders, etc., but also of **free** men; ἀ δήμου **one** of the people, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.198, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.352}}; with a qualifying word to indicate rank, ἀ. βουληφόρος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.61; ἀ. βασιλεύς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.253; ἡγήτορες ἄ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.687.}}
**II** **man,** opposed to **god,** πατὴρ ἀνδρῶν τε θεῶν τε {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.544, +others}}; Διὸς ἄγγελοι ἠδὲ καὶ ἀνδρῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 334, compare 403, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.63, etc.}}: most common in _plural_, yet sometimes in _singular_, e.g. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.432:—frequently with a Noun added, βροτοί, θνητοὶ ἄ., 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.197, 10.306; ἄ. ἡμίθεοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.23; ἄ. ἥρωες [prev. work] 5.746}}:—also of **men,** opposed to **monsters,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.303}}:—of **men** in societies and cities, οὔτε παρ᾽ ἀνδράσιν οὔτ᾽ ἐν ναυσὶ κοίλαις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.10}}; and so probably, ἄλλοτε μέν τ᾽ ἐπὶ Κύνθου ἐβήσαο.., ἄλλοτε δ᾽ ἂν νήσους τε καὶ ἀνέρας..{{Refs|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 142.}}
**III** **man,** opposed to **youth,** unless the context determines the meaning, as in οὔ πως ἔστι νεωτέρῳ ἀνδρὶ μάχεσθαι ἄνδρα γέροντα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.53}}; but ἀ. alone always means **a man in the prime of life,** especially **warrior,** ἀ. ἕλεν ἄνδρα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.328; so ἀ. ἀντ᾽ ἀνδρὸς ἐλύθησαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.103}}; the several ages are given as παῖς, μειράκιον, ἀ., πρεσβύτης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.17}}; εἰς ἄνδρας ἐγγράφεσθαι, συντελεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.230, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.212; εἰς ἄνδρας ἀναβῆναι “BMus.Inscription” 898}}; in Inscrr. relating to contests, opposed to παῖδες, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1138.10, etc.}}
**IV** **man** emphatically, **man indeed,** ἀνέρες ἄστε, φίλοι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.529; frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, πολλοὶ μὲν ἄνθρωποι, ὀλίγοι δὲ ἄνδρες 7.210; πρόσθεν οὐκ ἀ. ὅδ᾽ ἦ; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 77; ἄνδρα γίγνεσθαί σε χρή 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 693; ἀ. γεγένησαι δι᾽ ἐμέ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1255; ὃ μαθὼν ἀ. ἔσει [prev. author] “Nu.” 823; ἄνδρας ἡγοῦνται μόνους τοὺς πλεῖστα δυναμένους καταφαγεῖν [prev. author] “Ach.” 77; εἰ ἄνδρες εἶεν οἱ στρατηγοί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.27; οὐκέτι ἀ. ἀλλὰ σκευοφόρος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.25; τὸν Αυκομήδην.. μόνον ἄνδρα ἡγοῦντο [prev. author] “HG” 7.1.24}}; οὐκ ἐν ἀνδράσι not **like a man,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 723, compare 732}}; ἀνδρὸς τὰ προσπίπτοντα γενναίως φέρειν 'tis **the part of a man..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 771, etc.}}
**V** **husband,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.291, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.196, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.146, etc.; εἰς ἀνδρὸς ὥραν ἡκούσης τῆς κόρης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 113d; so ἐξοικιεῖν εἰς ἀνδρὸς [οἶκον] θυγατέρα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Legal.” 11}}:—also of **a paramour,** opposed to πόσις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 551, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 491, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.131; ἀ. ἁπασῶν τῶν γυναικῶν ἐστι νῦν 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 155; αἰγῶν ἄνερ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 8.49.}}
**VI** Special usages:
**VI.1** joined with titles, professions, etc., ἰητρὸς ἀ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.514}}; ἀ. μάντις, ἀ. στρατηγός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.83, 92 (uncertain); ἀ. νομεύς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1118; ἄνδρες λοχῖται, λῃσταί, ἀσπιστῆρες, [prev. work] 751, 842, “Aj.” 565; especially in disparagement, κλῶπες ἄ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 645; ἀ. δημότης 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 690}}; with names of nations, as Φοίνικες ἄ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.42; ἀ. Θρῇξ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 19, +others; especially in addresses, ἄ. ἔφοροι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.9; ἄ. πολῖται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 513; ἄ. δικασταί 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.1, etc.}}; ὦ ἄνδρες **gentlemen** of the jury, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.1, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.1, etc.; ὦ ἄ. Ἀθηναῖοι [prev. author] 6.8, etc.}}: hence in Comedy, ἄ. ἰχθύες{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 29; ἄ. θεοί 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 15; ὦ ἄ. κύνες 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.160b.}}
**VI.2** ὁ ἀνήρ, by crasis _Attic dialect_ ἁνήρ, _Ionic dialect_ ὡνήρ, is frequently used emphatically for αὐτός, ἐκεῖνος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 269, probably in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 216b, etc.}}: sometimes so in oblique cases without the _Article_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 55, 109, 293, etc.}}; but not in Prose.
**VI.3** ἀ. ὅδε, ὅδ᾽ ἀ., in Trag., ={ἐγώ}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 78, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 690, etc.}}
**VI.4** πᾶς ἀ. every **man,** every **one,** frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 736c, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1523.}}
**VI.5** **a man, any man,** εἶτ᾽ ἄνδρα τῶν αὑτοῦ τι χρὴ προϊένα;{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1214; οὐ πρέπει νοῦν ἔχοντι ἀνδρί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 114d, etc.}}; οὐ παντὸς ἀνδρὸς.. ἐσθ᾽ ὁ πλοῦς 'tis not **every one** that can go, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC(?): Nicolaus Comicus 1.26.}}
**VI.6** ὦ δαιμόνι᾽ ἀνδρῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 316}}; and often with a _superlative_, ὦ φίλτατ᾽ ἀνδρῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 80, etc.}}
**VI.7** κατ᾽ ἄνδρα **viritim,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.180, “POxy.” 1047 iii 11, “BGU” 145.5, etc.}}; so τοὺς κατ᾽ ἄνδρα **individuals,** opposed to κοινῇ τὴν πόλιν, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 32.6.}}
**VI.8** In [{{LXX|LXX, ἀνήρ ={ἕκαστος, δότε μοι ἀνὴρ ἐνώτιον} LXX.Judg.8.24}}]; ἀ. τῷ ἀδελφῷ αὐτοῦ προσκολληθήσεται **'each to his fellow',** of leviathan's scales, {{LXX|LXX.Job.41.8; also ἀ. εἷς LXX.4Ki.6.2}}; with _negatives_, ἀ. μὴ ἐπισκεπήτω{{Refs|[prev. work] 10.19; ἀνὴρ ἀνήρ}}**any one, Le.** {{Refs|15.2.}}
**VI.9** ἄνδρας γράφειν· τὸ ἐν διδασκάλου τὰ παιδία ὀνόματα γράφειν, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**VII** **male animal,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 637b15.}}

436:
** ἀνθ-ίστημι**,
**set against,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.115; especially in battle, πελταστικὴν τῆ τοῦ παγκρατίου μάχῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 834a}}; ἀ. τροπαῖον **set up** a trophy **in opposition,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.54, 105}}; **weigh against,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1389}}; ἀ. τινὸς τὴν ὁλκήν **outweigh** him, {{LXX|LXX.Sir. 8.2.}}
**2** **match with, compare,** ἀντιστῆσαι καὶ παραβαλεῖν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 1.}}
**II** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses only}}_passive_, with _intransitive_ _aorist 2_ ἀντέστην. _aorist 1_ _passive_ ἀντεστάθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.72}}: _perfect_ ἀνθέστηκα{{NT|NT.Rom.9.19}}; _Attic dialect_ _contraction_ _participle_ ἀνθεστώς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.70}}: _future_ ἀντιστήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.75, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 645}}:—**stand against,** especially in battle, **withstand,** Ἥρῃ δ᾽ ἀντέστη.. Ἄρτεμις{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.70, compare 72, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.117 +others; τοὺς ἀνθισταμένους τοῖς ὑμετέροις βουλήμασι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.49; πρὸς τὴν ἀνάγκην οὐδ᾽ Ἄρης ἀ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 256, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 5.1: rarely with _genitive_, δέος.. σοὶ φρενῶν ἀνθίσταται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 703 (ἀνθάπτεται Wakef.),compare 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.520.}}
**II.2** of things, **turn out unfavourably** to one, ἀντιστάντος αὐτῷ τοῦ πράγματος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.4, compare 38; ἄν τὰ παρ᾽ ὑμῶν τῶν ἀκουόντων ἀντιστῇ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.340.}}
**II.3** _absolutely_, **make a stand,** ἀλλ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἀνθίσταντο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.305}}; **resist, fight on,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.72, etc.; ὑπέρ τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1231, “Ant.” 518.}}

437:
** ἀνθομολογ-έομαι**,
**make a mutual agreement** or **covenant,** πρός τινα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.8(assuming variant), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.105.2; ὑπέρ τινος 15.19.9; τινί “PTeb.” 21.6(2nd c.BC); περί τινος [prev. work] 410.14 (16 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus).}}
**II** **confess freely and openly,** τὰς ἀρετάς τινος{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.70; ἁμαρτίας 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.10.3; τὸν τοῦ βασιλέως θάνατον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.25.4: _absolutely_, 30.8.7.}}
**II.2** **admit, signify,** πρός τινα μηδὲν ἑωρακέναι{{Refs 1st c.AD+|29.17.1; ὡς.. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 16.}}
**II.3** **assent, agree,** τοῖς εἰρημένοις{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 28.4.4.}}
**II.4** **return thanks** to God, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.78(79).13. NT.Luke.2.38}}; χάριν ἀ. **return** thanks, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 11:}} —_active_, -λογέω **admit a claim,** is late, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PGrenf.” 2.71i i14 (3rd c.AD).}}

438:
** ἄνθος**, ὁ,
a kind of
**B** **bird,** perhaps **the yellow wagtail,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 592b25, 609b14, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 5.48.}}

439:
** ἀνθρᾰκ-ιά**, ᾶς,
_Epic dialect_ **ἀνθρᾰκ-ιή**, ῆς, ἡ, **burning charcoal, hot embers,** ἀνθρακιὴν στορέσαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.213; ὑποθεῖναι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 61}}; ἀνθρακιᾶς ἄπο a broil hot from **the embers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 358, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 6.105 (1st c.AD: Apollonides Epigrammaticus); ἐπ᾽ ἀνθρακιᾶς ὀπτῆσαι 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 143}}; σου τῆς ἀνθρακιᾶς ἀπολαύει warms himself at your **fire,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 780: _metaphorically_ of lovers, τιθέναι τινὰ ὑπὸ ἀνθρακιῆ or ἀνθρακιήν “Anthologia Graeca” 12.17,166 (3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus)}}; Κύπριδος ἀ.{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 5.210 (3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus).}}
**2** **black sooty ashes,**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 11.66 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus).}}

440:
** ἄνθραξ**, ᾰκος, ὁ,
**charcoal,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Sotades Comicus 1.12}}: mostly in _plural_ ἄνθρακες{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 34, 332, “Nu.” 97; ἄ. Παρνήθιοι [prev. author] “Ach.” 348; ὀπτωμέναις κόγχαισιν ἐπί τῶν ἀ. [prev. author] “fragment” 68; ἄνθρακας ἡμμένους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.100, etc.}}; their vapour produced stupor, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Sensu” 444b31: proverbial, ἄνθρακας κατεσθίειν}} of a glutton, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 10.14, cf. Ion Trag. 29.}}
**2** **coal,** οἷς καὶ οἱ χαλκεῖς χρῶνται{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 16, “PHolm.” 2.33}}; ἐπὶ ἀνθράκων μαλακῶν on a slow **fire,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Xenocrates Medicus 16.}}
**II** a precious stone of dark-red colour, including the **carbuncle, ruby,** and **garnet** {{LXX+4th c.BC+|(from Adams), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 387b18, LXX.Exo.28.18, 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 41, etc.}}
**II.2** hence, **carbuncle, malignant pustule** (_accusative_ to some, **small-pox**), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 3.7, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.719, +others}}
**III** ={ἰσάτις}, **woad,** {{Refs|PHolm. 18.34, +others}}

442:
** ἀνθρώπ-ινος**, η, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 737b}}:—
**A** **of, from,** or **belonging to man, human,** ἀ. βίος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 11, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.46}}; ἅπαν τὸ ἀ. all **mankind,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.86}}; τὸ ἀ. γένος (variant{φῦλον}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 82b}}; ἀ. κίνδυνοι, opposed to θεῖοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.139; ἀ. δίκη 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.20}}; ἀ. τεκμήρια, opposed to omens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.81; τἀνθρώπινα}}**human affairs,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 170b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1102b3 (variant{-ικά}) ἀνθρώπινόν τι παθεῖν die, “IG” 5(2), 266.20 (Mantinea, 1st c.BC),compare “PPetr.” 1p.33 (3rd c.BC), “PRyl.” 153.39 (2nd c.AD)}}]; so ἐάν τι τῶν ἀ. περί τινα γένηται{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 217.}}
**A.2** **human, suited to man,** ἀνθρωπίνη δόξα **fallible, human** understanding, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 229a}}; οὐκ ἀ. ἀμαθία **super-human, monstrous** folly, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 737b, etc.}}; ἀ. καὶ μετρία σκῆψις
**D**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|21.41; οὐ χρὴ ἀνθρώπινα φρονεῖν ἄνθρωπον ὄντα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1177b32; ἀ. νοῦς 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 482; ἀ. τὸ γεγενημένον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.19.}}
**D.3** ἀνθρώπινα, τά, **secular revenues**, {{Refs|“SIG” 527.133}}; **secular rites,** opposed to θῖνα, {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 10.43.}}
**D.II** _adverb_ ἀνθρωπίνως, ἁμαρτάνειν commit **human,** i.e. **venial,** errors, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40; ἀνθρωπινώτερον}}**more within the range of human faculty,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 392b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.252}}; ἀνθρωπίνως ἐκλογίζεσθαι, i.e. **with fellow-feeling,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.6}}; **humanely, gently,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.70; ἀ. χρὴ τὰς τύχας φέρειν}}**with moderation,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 816; εὐτυχίαν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.60.}} —Of the three forms, ἀνθρώπειος is used exclusively in Trag. and generally in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus (but compare 1.22)}}; ἀνθρώπινος prevails in Comedy and in Prose from {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus downwards (though he uses ἀνθρώπειος no less frequently)}}; ἀνθρωπικός is frequently in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}} **ἀνθρώπ-ιον**, τό, = {ἀνθρωπίσκος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 185, 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 34}}; **paltry fellow,** ὦ πόνηρ᾽ ἀνθρώπια{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 263, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.16, “Cyr.” 5.1.14, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.242.}}

443:
** ἀνθρωπο-κτόνος**, ον,
**murdering men, homicide,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 389, NT.1John.3.15, NT.John.8.44.}}
**II** _proparoxytone_, ἀνθρωπόκτονος βορά feeding **on slaughtered men,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 127.}}

444:
** ἄνθρωπος**, ἡ,
_Attic dialect_ crasis ἅνθρωπος, _Ionic dialect_ ὥνθρωπος, for ὁ ἄνθρ-:— **man,** both as a generic term and of individuals, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus etc.}}, opposed to **gods,** ἀθανάτων τε θεῶν χαμαὶ ἐρχομένων τ᾽ ἀνθρώπων{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.442, etc.}}; πρὸς ἠοίων ἢ ἑσπερίων ἀνθρώπων **the men** of the east or of the west, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.29}}; even of the dead in the Isles of the Blest,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 4.565; κόμπος οὐ κατ᾽ ἄνθρωπον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 425, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 761.}}
**2** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}} uses it both with and without the _Article_ to denote **man** generically, ὁ ἄ. θείας μετέσχε μοίρας{{Refs|“Prt.” 322a; οὕτω.. εὐδαιμονέστατος γίγνεται ἄ. “R.” 619b, +others}}; ὁ ἄ. **the ideal man, humanity,** ἀπώλεσας τὸν ἄ., οὐκ ἐπλήρωσας τὴν ἐπαγγελίαν{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.9.3.}}
**3** in _plural_, **mankind,** ἀνθρώπων.. ἀνδρῶν ἠδὲ γυναικῶν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.134; ἐν τῷ μακρῷ.. ἀνθρώπων χρόνῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 306}}; ἐξἀνθρώπων γίγνεσθαι depart **this life,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 4.26.5, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 8.31.}}
**3.b** joined with a _superlative_ to increase its force, δεινότατον τῶν ἐν ἀνθρώποις ἁπάντων{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.2}}; ὁ ἄριστος ἐν ἀνθρώποις ὄρτυξ the best quail in **the world,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 211e}}; frequently without a _preposition_, μάλιστα, ἥκιστα ἀνθρώπων, most or least **of all,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 629a, “Prt.” 361e}}; ἄριστά γ᾽ ἀ., ὀρθότατα ἀ., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 148b, 195b, etc.}}
**3.c** τὰ ἐξ ἀνθρώπων πράγματα 'all the trouble in the world',{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 170e; γραφὰς τὰς ἐξ ἀνθρώπων ἐγράφετο 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.73; αἱ ἐξ ἀνθρώπων πληγαί 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.59; πάντα τὰ ἐξ ἀνθρώπων κακὰ ἔλεγε 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 57.23.}}
**4** joined with another _substantive_, like{ἀνήρ}, ἄ. ὁδίτης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.263; πολίτας ἀ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.54}}; with names of nations, πόλις Μερόπων ἀνθρώπων{{Refs|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 42}}; in _Attic dialect_ frequently in a contemptuous sense, ἄ. ὑπογραμματεύς, ἄ. γόης, ἄ. συκοφάντης, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.28, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.153,183; ἄ. ἀλαζών 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.7.2; ἄ. ὑφάντης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 87b; Μενίππου, Καρός τινος ἀνθρώπου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.175; ἄ. βασιλεύς NT.Matt.22.2.}}
**5** ἅνθρωπος or ὁ ἄνθρωπος alone, **the man, the fellow,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314e, “Phd.” 117e; ὡς ἀστεῖος ὁ ἄ., with slight irony, [prev. work] 116d, +others}}; with a sense of pity, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.91.}}
**6** in the _vocative_ frequently in a contemptuous sense, as when addressed to slaves, etc., ἄνθρωπε or ὤνθρωπε **sirrah! you sir!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.63, 8.125, and frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, but in Trag. only 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 791, 1154}}; simply, **brother,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 215.1, 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 2.}}
**7** **slave,** ἂν ἄ. ᾖ{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 22; ἄ. ἐμός 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.649}}; ὁ ἄ. τῆς ἁμαρτίας or ἀνομίας{{NT|NT.2Thes.2.3; ἄ. τοῦ Θεοῦ NT.1Tim.6.11}}; but τιθέναι τινὰ ἐν ἀνθρώποις make **a man of,** of a freed slave, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.15.}}
**8** ἄ. ἄ. **any one,** Hebraism in {{LXX|LXX.Lev.17.3 (compare ἀνήρ VI. 8)}}; ἄ. like [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|\{1st c.BC-1st c.AD\}: Germanicus Caesar Epigrammaticus}}] **man** 'one', {{NT|NT.1Cor.4.1, +others}}
**9** in Medicine texts, name of a **plaster,** ἡ διὰ σάνδυκος ἄ. καλουμένη{{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 15.43.}}
**II** as _feminine_, **woman,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.98, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.52, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1148b20}}; contemptuously, of female slaves, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.17, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.20, etc.}}; with a sense of pity, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.197.}} —Prop. opposed to θηρίον, compare ἀνή; but opposed to γυνή, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.137; ἀπὺ ἀνθρώπου ἕως γυναικός LXX.1Esd.9.40, etc.}}

446:
** ἀνθῠπᾰτ-ος**, ὁ,
**proconsul,** Latin _pro consule,_ {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 28.5.6, NT.Act.13.7, etc., frequently in Inscrr. as “SIG” 684.3, and Papyrus as “Sammelb.” 3924.32 (1st c.AD)}}; στρατηγὸς ἀ.{{Refs|“SIG” 704 K, etc.}}
**II** as _adjective_, **proconsular,** ἐξουσία{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.16; ἀρχή [prev. author] 11.62.}}

447:
** ἀνίημι**, ης
(ἀνιεῖς, as if from ἀνιέω, uncertain in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.880)}}, ησι: _imperfect_ ἀνίην, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and _Attic dialect_ 2}} and _3rd.pers.singular_ εις, ει, _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers.singular_ ἀνίη{{Refs 8th c.BC+|“SIG” 1 (Abu Simbel, 6th c.BC, Iterat. ἀνίεσκε 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 157; also ἠνίει 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.46}}; _1st pers.singular_ ἀνίειν{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 4:}}_future_ ἀνήσω: _perfect_ ἀνεῖκα: _aorist 1_ ἀνῆκ; _Ionic dialect_ ἀνέηκα.:—the Homeric forms ἀνέσει{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.265}}, _aorist_ _optative_ ἀνέσαιμι{{Refs|14.209}}, _participle_ ἀνέσαντες{{Refs 8th c.BC+|13.657 should be referred to ἀνέζω, but ἄνεσαν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.537 is from ἀνίημι}}: _aorist 2_, _3rd.pers.plural_ ἀνεῖσαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.32, _imperative_ ἄνες 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 489, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1101, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 442}}, _subjunctive_ ἀνῇς{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 183}}, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers.singular_ _subjunctive_ ἀνήη{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.34, _optative_ ἀνείη}}_infinitive_ ἀνεῖναι, _participle_ ἀνείς:—_passive_, ἀνίεμαι: _perfect_ ἀνεῖμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.65, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 413}}, _3rd.pers.plural_ _perfect_ ἀνέωνται[{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.165 (variant{ἀνέονται})}}]_infinitive_ ἀνἑῶσθαι (sic) {{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.153}}: _aorist_ _participle_ ἀνεθείς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 41c e}}: _future_ ἀνεθήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.63. [ἀνῐ- _Epic dialect_, ἀνῑ-}} _Attic dialect_: but even {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has ἀνῑει, ἀνῑέμενος, and we find ἀνῐησιν in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 153 (_anapaest meter_)}}.]: —**send up** or **forth,** Ζεφύροιο.. ἀήτας Ὠκεανὸς ἀνίησιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.568}}; of Charybdis, τρὶς μὲν γάρ τ᾽ ἀνίησιν.. τρὶς δ᾽ ἀναροιβδεῖ{{Refs|12.105; ἀφρὸν ἀ.}}**spew up, vomit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 183; σταγόνας [αἵματος] ἀ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1277}}; of the earth, καρπὸν ἀ. **make** corn or fruit **spring up,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Hymnus in Cererem 333; κνώδαλα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 266}}; also of the gods, ἀ. ἄροτον γῆς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 270, etc.}}; so of females, **produce,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 1405}}:—in _passive_, σπαρτῶν ἀπ᾽ ἀνδρῶν ῥίζωμ᾽ ἀνεῖται{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 413}}: then in various relations, συὸς χρῆμα ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 401; κρήνην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 766}}; of a forest, πῦρ καὶ φλόγα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.77; πνεῦμ᾽ ἀνεὶς ἐκ πνευμόνων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 277}}:—**send up** from the grave or nether world, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 650, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1462, 5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 1 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 403e, etc.:}} — _passive_, ἐκ γῆς κάτωθεν ἀνίεται ὁ πλοῦτος{{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; of fruit, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.1.5.}}
**2** **let come up, give access to,** τινά{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.11; εἰς τὸ πεδίον [prev. work] 7.2.12.}}
**II** **let go,** from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards a very common sense, ἐμὲ δὲ γλυκὺς ὕπνος ἀνῆκεν}} i.e. **left** me, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.71, etc., compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310d}}: —_passive_, ἀνίεσθαι **wake up,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.56}}; **set free,** ἐκ στέγης ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1101}}; **let go unpunished,** ἄνδρα τὴν ὀλιγαρχίαν λυμαινόμενον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.51, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.93}}; ἄνετέ μ᾽ ἄνετε **leave** me **alone, forbear,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 229 (Lyric poetry)}}; of a state of mind, ἐμὲ δ᾽ οὐδ᾽ ὣς θυμὸν ἀνίει.. ὀδύνη{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.24; ὅταν μ᾽ ἀνῇ νόσος μανίας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 227; ὥς μιν ὁ οἶνος ἀνῆκε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.213, etc.}}; ἀ. ἵππον **to let** him **go** (by slackening the rein), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 721; ἵππους εἰς τάχος ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 3.2; τῷ δήμῳ τὰς ἡνίας ἀ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 11.}}
**II.b** **loosen, unfasten,** δεσμόν[{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.359 (variant{δεσμῶν}); δεσμά τ᾽ ἀνεῖσαι 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hecale” 1.2.13}}]: hence, **open,** πύλας ἄνεσαν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.537; ἀ. θύρετρα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 448}}; ἀ. σήμαντρα **break** the seal, {{Refs|[prev. author] “IA” 325}}:—_passive_, πύλαι ἀνειμέναι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 10.14.}}
**II.2** ἀ. τινί **let loose at** one, **slip at,** ἀ. τὰς κύνας{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 7.7: hence ἄφρονα τοῦτον ἀνέντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.761, compare 880: with _accusative_ et}}_infinitive_, Διομήδεα μαργαίνειν ἀνέηκεν{{Refs|[prev. work] 882}}: generally, **set on or urge** to do a thing, with _infinitive_, Μοῦσ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἀοιδὸν ἀνῆκεν ἀειδέμεναι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.73, compare 17.425, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.276, 5.422}}: frequently with _accusative_ _person_ only, **let loose, excite,** as οὐδέ κε Τηλέμαχον.. ῷδ᾽ ἀνιείης{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.185; μέγας δέ σε θυμὸς ἀνῆκεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.25}}; τοῖσιν μὲν Θρασυμήδεα δῖον ἀνῆκεν **urged** Thrasymedes **to** their **aid,** {{Refs|17.705}}:—so in _passive_, ἅπας κίνδυνος ἀνεῖται σοφίας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 955.}}
**II.3** ἀ. τινὰ πρός τι **to let go for** any purpose, τὸν λεὼν.. ἀνεῖναι πρὸς ἔργα τε καὶ θυσίας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.129}}; ἐς παιγνίην ἑωυτὸν ἀ.[{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 173; τὰ μικρὰ εἰς τύχην ἀνείς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 974 (variant{ἀφείς}); τὰ σώματα ἐπὶ ῥᾳδιουργίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.75}}]; ἐὰν δ᾽ ἀνῇς, ὕβριστον χρῆμα κἀκόλαστον [γυνή] if you **leave** her **free,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 98.}}
**II.4** **let, allow,** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ἀνεῖναι αὐτοὺς ὅ τι βούλονται ποιεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 179a; ἀ. τρίχας αὔξεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.36, compare 4.175}}: with _infinitive_ omitted, ἀνεῖσα πένθει κόμαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 323}}; ἀ. στολίδος κροκόεσσαν τρυφάν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 1491; κόμας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lysander” 1: with _dative_ _person_ et}}_infinitive_, ἀνεὶς αὐτῷ θηρᾶν **having given** him **leave** to hunt, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.3.}}
**II.5** _middle_, **loosen, undo,** with _accusative_, κόλπον ἀνιεμένη **baring her** breast, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.80}}; αἶγας ἀνιέμενοι **stripping** or **flaying** goats, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.300; so ἀνεῖτο λαγόνας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 826}}; so in _active_, ἀνιέναι· δέρειν, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II.6** **let go free, leave untilled,** of ground dedicated to a god, τέμενος ἀνῆκεν ἅπαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.116; ἀργὸν παντάπασι τὸ χωρίον ἀνιέντες τῷ θεῷ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 8; generally, τὴν χώραν ἀ. μηλόβοτον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.31; ἀρούρας ἀσπόρους ἀ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.11.9; στέλεχος ἀνειμένον}}**allowed to run wild,** {{LXX|LXX.Gen.49.21}}:—but this sense mostly in _passive_, **devote oneself, give oneself up,** ἐς τὸ ἐλεύθερον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.103}}; especially of animals dedicated to a god, which **are let range at large** (compare ἄνετος), ἀνεῖται τὰ θηρία{{Refs|[prev. author] 2.65}}; of a person **devoted to** the gods, νῦν δ᾽ οὗτος ἀνεῖται στυγερῷ δαίμονι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1214}}; of places, etc., θεοῖσιν ἀ. δένδρεα{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Cererem” 47}}; ἄλσος ἀνειμένον a **consecrated** grove, conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 761c}}; of land, ἀ. εἰς νομάς{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 60.8, 72.36 (2nd c.BC): hence _metaphorically_, ἀνειμένος εἴς τι}} **devoted to** a thing, **wholly engaged in** it, e.g. ἐς τὸν πόλεμον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.167}}; ἀνέωνται ἐς τὸ μάχιμον they **are given up** to military service, {{Refs|[prev. work] 165}}; ἐς τὸ κέρδος λῆμ᾽ ἀνειμένον **given up** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 3: hence}}_perfect_ _participle_ _passive_ ἀνειμένος as _adjective_, **going free, left to one's own will and pleasure, at large,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 579, “El.” 516; ἀ. τι χρῆμα πρεσβυτῶν γένος καὶ δυσφύλακτον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 727}}; πέπλοι ἀνειμένοι **let hang loose,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 598}}; τὸ εἰς ἀδικίαν καὶ πλεονεξίαν -μένον **unrestrained propensity to**.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 16; σώματα πρὸς πᾶσαν ἐπιθυμίαν ἀνειμένα [prev. author] “Lyc.” 10.}}
**II.7** **slacken, relax,** opposed to ἐπιτείνω or ἐντείνω, of a bow or stringed instrument, **unstring,** as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.22, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 442a, “Ly.” 209b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.7, etc.}}; especially of musical scales, ἁρμονίαι ἀνειμέναι, opposed to σύντονοι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1342b22, +others; ἀνειμένα Ἰαστὶ μοῦσα Pratin.Lyric poetry 5}}: _metaphorically_, ὀργῆς ὀλίγον τὸν κόλλοπ᾽ ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 574, compare 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 145.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 410e; πολιτεῖαι ἀνειμέναι καὶ μαλακαί 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1290a28; τοῖς γηράσκουσι ἀνίεται ἡ συντονία “GA” 787b13}}; ἀνειμένη τάσις the **grave** accent,{{Refs|Scholia 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus p.130H.; οἱ πάγοι τὰς φλόγας ἀ.}}**temper,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 397b2: hence}}
**II.7.b** **remit, neglect, give up,** στέρνων ἀραγμούς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1608; φυλακὰς ἀνῆκα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1042}}; φυλακήν, ἄσκησιν, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.27, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.70, etc.}}; ἀ. θάνατόν τινι **to remit** sentence of death to one, let one live, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 531; ἔχθρας, κολάσεις τισί 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.536a}}; ἀ. τὰ χρέα, τὰς καταδίκας, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Sol.” 15, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 64.8, compare 72.2}}; ἄνες λόγον speak **more mildly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 442; so ἀ. τινὸς ἔχθραν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.10}}; ἀ. ἀρχήν, πόλεμον, etc., {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.76, 7.18, etc.}}:—_passive_, **to be treated remissly,** ἀνεθήσεται τὰ πράγματα{{Refs|[prev. author] 8.63; ὁ νόμος ἀνεῖται}}**has become effete, powerless,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 941: frequently in}}_perfect_ _participle_ ἀνειμένος as an _adjective_, ἐν τῷ ἀνειμένῳ τῆς γνώμης when their minds are **not strung up for action,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.9}}; ἀνειμένῃ τῇ διαίτῃ **relaxed, unconstrained,** of the Athenians, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.6}}; δίαιτα λίαν ἀ., of the Ephors, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1270b32; ἀ. ἡδοναί}}**dissolute,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 573a}}; ἄνανδρος καὶ λίαν ἀ.{{Refs|[prev. work] 549d; ἀ. χείλεα}}**parched,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.63}}; of climate, ἀ. καὶ μαλακός{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.4.4; ὀσμὴ μαλακὴ καὶ ἀ. 5.7.1}}: _comparative_ ἀνειμενώτερος{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 15.67:}} —but,
**II.8** the sense of **relaxation** occurs also as an _intransitive_ usage of the _active_, **slacken, abate,** of the wind, ἐπειδὰν πνεῦμ᾽ ἀνῇ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 639, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.113, 4.152; ἕως ἀνῇ τὸ πῆμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 764, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.94}}; ἐμφῦσα οὐκ ἀνίει, of a viper, having fastened on him she **does** not **let go,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.109: especially in phrase οὐδὲν ἀνιέναι}} not **to give way** at all, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.46, compare “Cyr.” 1.4.22}}; τὰς τιμὰς ἀνεικέναι ἤκουον that prices **had fallen,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.25, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1390a15}}; σιδήρια ἀ. ἐν τοῖς μαλακοῖς **lose** their **edge,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.5.1.}}
**II.8.b** with _participle_, **give up** or **cease** doing, ὕων οὐκ ἀνίει [ὁ θεός] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.28, compare 125, 2.121.β, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 318, etc.}}
**II.8.c** with _genitive_, **cease from** a thing, μωρίας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Med.” 457; τῆς ὀργῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 700, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.186; φιλονικίας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.32}}; ἀνῆκε τοῦ ἐξελθεῖν **forbore to** come forth, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.23.13.}}
**II.9** **dilute, dissolve,** διά τινος or τινί, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.520, +others, “Geoponica” 4.7.3, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 7.20.5 (2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 19 says that διΐημι is more correct in this sense)}}; διυγραινομένων καὶ ἀνιεμένων{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Ventis” 58.}}

449:
** ἄνιπτος**, ον,
**unwashen,** χερσὶ δ᾽ ἀνίπτοισιν (variant{-ῃσιν}) Διὶ λείβειν.. ἅξομαι{{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.266, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 725, NT.Matt.15.20: proverbial, ἀ. ποσί}} i.e. **unprepared,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pseudol.” 4.}}
**2** **not to be washed out,** αἷμα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1459.}}

450:
** ἀνίστημι**,
causal in _present_ ἀνίστημι (later ἀνιστάω {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.61):}}_imperfect_ ἀνίστην: _future_ ἀναστήσω, _poetry_ ἀνστήσω: _aorist 1_ ἀνέστησα, _Epic dialect_ ἄνστησα, _Aeolic dialect_ _3rd.pers.plural_ ὄστασαν{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}: _perfect_ ἀνέστακα{{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.1Ki.15.12, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.4.30: also in}}_aorist 1_ _middle_ ἀνεστησάμην (see. below {{Refs|1.5, 111.6).}}
**I** **make to stand up, raise up,** γέροντα δὲ χειρὸς ἀνίστη he **raised** the old man **up** by his hand, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.515, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.319; τί μ᾽ αὖ.. ἐξ ἑδρας ἀνίστατ; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 788; ἀ. τινὰ ἐκ τῆς κλίνης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317e; ὀρθὸν ἀ. τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.11; ἀπὸ τοῦ καθαρμοῦ τινα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.259.}}
**I.2** **raisefrom sleep, wake up,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.32, etc.; εἰς ἐκκλησίαν ἀ. τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 740; ἀ. τινὰ ὠμόϋπνον 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 305: _metaphorically_, ἀ. νόσον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 979.}}
**I.3** **raise from the dead,** οὐδέ μιν ἀνστήσεις{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.551, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1361, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 139}}; from misery or misfortune, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 666, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.67.}}
**I.4** **produce** a witness, etc. (compare 111.6), προφήτην ὑμῖν ἀ. ὁ Θεός{{NT|NT.Act.3.22, +others}}
**I.5** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} also of things, **set up, build,** στήλας variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.102; πύργους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.12, etc.; τρόπαια Διί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 572}}; ἀνδριάντα ἐς Δελφούς Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.21}}; so ἀ. τινὰ χρυσοῦν, χαλκοῦν (in pure Attic ἱστάναι), **set up a** golden, brazen **statue** of him, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.170e, “Brut.” 1 (_passive_, see infr. B):}} —so in _aorist 1_ _middle_, ἀναστήσασθαι πόλιν **build oneself** a city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.165}}; ἀνεστήσαντο δὲ βωμούς **they set them up** altars, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 199.}}
**I.5.b** **build up again, restore,** τείχη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.68: _metaphorically_, θεῶν τιμάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 852.}}
**I.6** **put up for sale,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.196.}}
**II** **rouse to action, stir up,** ἀλλ᾽ ἴθι νῦν Αἴαντα.. ἄνστησον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.176, compare 179, 15.64}}, etc.: with _dative_ _person_, **raise up against** another, τούτῳ δὲ πρόμον ἄλλον ἀναστήσουσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 7.116 (see. below 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.5)}}: **rouse to arms, raise** troops, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.68, 96; ἀ. πόλεμον ἐπί τινα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 21}}; ἀναστήσας ἦγε στρατόν he **called up** his troops and marched them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.93, compare 112, etc.}}
**III** **make** people **rise, break up** an assembly **by force,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.191}}; but ἐκκλησίαν ἀναστῆσαι **adjourn** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.42.}}
**III.2** **make** people **emigrate, transplant** (compare below {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.2), ἔνθεν ἀναστήσας ἄγε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.7; ἀνίστασαν τοὺς δήμους 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.73; Αἰγινήτας ἐξ Αἰγίνης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.27; even γαῖαν ἀναστήσειν 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1349; οἴκους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 21; also ἀ. τινὰ ἐκ τῆς ἐργασίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.129.}}
**III.3** **make** suppliants **rise and leave sanctuary,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.71, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 276, etc.: also ἀ. στρατόπεδον ἐκ χώρας}} **make** an army **decamp,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 29.27.10; τὰ πράγματα ἀνίστησί τινα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 31.}}
**III.4** ἀ. ἐπὶ τὸ βῆμα **make to ascend** the tribune, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.784c, compare “Cam.” 32.}}
**III.5** of sportsmen, **put up** game, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.3, compare “Cyr.” 2.4.20 (_passive_), “Cyn.” 6.23, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 2.2.}}
**III.6** μάρτυρα ἀναστής ασθαί τινα **call** him **as one's** witness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 937a.}}
**B** _intransitive_ in _present_ and _imperfect_ ἀνίσταμαι, -μην, in _future_ ἀναστήσομαι, in _aorist 2_ ἀνέστην (but ἀναστῶ, for ἀναστήσω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 4D.), _imperative_ ἄστηθι (for ἄν-στηθι) 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 8.1}}, _participle_ ἀστάς{{Refs|“IG” 4.951.112 (Epid.):}}_perfect_ ἀνέστηκα, _Attic dialect_ _pluperfect_ ἀνεστήκ; also _perfect_ ἀνεστέασι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.62:}}_aorist_ _passive_ ἀνεστάθην, _Aeolic dialect_ _participle_ ὀσταθείς{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}:—**stand up, rise,** especially to speak, τοῖσι δ᾽ ἀνέστη{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.68, 101, etc.; ἐν μέσσοισι 19.77: in _Attic dialect_ with}}_future_ _participle_, ἀ. λέξων, κατηγορήσων, etc.: so with _infinitive_, ἀνέστη μαντεύεσθαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.380}}: in _participle_, ἀναστὰς εἶπε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 885; παραινέσεις ἐποιοῦντο ἐν σφίσιν αὐτοῖς ἀνιστάμενοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.76}}; also, **rise from one's seat** as a mark of respect, θεοὶ δ᾽ ἅμα πάντες ἀνέσταν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.533}}; ἀπὸ βωμοῦ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111.3) 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.84.}}
**B.2** **rise from bed** or **sleep,** ἐξ εὐνῆς ἀνστᾶσα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.336, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 124; εὐνῆθεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.124; ὄρθρου ἀ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 577; ὀψέ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 217}}; οὐδ᾽ ἀνιστάμην ἐκ κλίνης, of a sick person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.64}}: _absolutely_, **rise from sleep,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31.}}
**B.3** **rise from the dead,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.56, compare 15.287, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.62, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 569; παρὰ τῶν πλειόνων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1073.}}
**B.4** **rise from** an illness, **recover,** ἐκ τῆς νούσου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 195c: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49.}}
**B.5** **rise** as a champion, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.709}}; θανάτων χώρᾳ πύργος ἀνέστα [Oedipus] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1201}}: hence with _dative_, **stand up** [to fight against..], Ἀγκαῖον.., ὅς μοι ἀνέστη{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.635; μή τίς τοι.. ἄλλος ἀναστῇ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.334; Τυφῶνα θοῦρον πᾶσιν ὃς ἀνέστη θεοῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 354 codices}}; see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.}}
**B.6** **rise up, rear itself,** πύργοι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 824 (Lyric poetry), compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.1.5}}; of statues, etc., **to be set up,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.91a, 198f: _metaphorically_, μή τι ἐξ αὐτῶν ἀναστήῃ κακόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.155; πόλεμος 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.23; θορύβου ἀναστάντος 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.56.}}
**B.7** **to be set up,** βασιλεύς as king, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.66 codices}}
**B.8** of a river, **rise,** ἐξ ὀρέων{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 34.}}
**B.9** _perfect_ _participle_, γῆ γηλόφοισιν ἀνεστηκυῖα{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Indica” 4.7}}: _metaphorically_, **lofty,** ἀ. τὴν ψυχὴν γενόμενος{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.233 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**B.II** **rise to go, set out, go away,** εἰς Ἄργος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 59, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.87, 7.49, 50; ἀνίστατο εἰς οἴκημά τι ὡς λουσόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116a.}}
**B.II.2** **to be compelled to migrate** (above {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111.2), ἐξ Ἄρνης ἀναστάντες ὑπὸ Θεσσαλῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.12, compare 8}}: of a country, **to be depopulated,** χώρα ἀνεστηκυῖα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.29; πόλις.. πᾶσ᾽ ἀνέστηκεν δορί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 494}}; ἡσυχάσασα ἡ Ἑλλὰς καὶ οὐκέτι ἀνισταμένη no longer **subject to migration,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.12; τὴν ἀσφάλειαν.. περιείδετ᾽ ἀνασταθεῖσαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.84.}}
**B.II.3** of a law-court, **rise,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 21.221.}}
**B.II.4** **cease,** οὐκ, ἀνέστη ἕως ἐνίκησε σκορπίσαι{{Refs|“Psalms of Solomon [1st c.BC]” 4.13.}}

453:
** ἀνό-ητος**, ον,
**not thought on, unheard of,** ἄφραστ᾽ ἠδ᾽ ἀνόητα{{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 80.}}
**2** **not within the province of thought,** νοήματα ὄντα ἀνόητα εἶναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 132c}}; **not the object of thought, unthinkable,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.3.6 and 10.}}_adverb_ -τως **without discursive thought,** of vision, βλέψαι ἀ.{{Refs|[prev. author] 6.7.16.}}
**II** _active_, **not understanding, unintelligent, senseless, silly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87, 8.24}}; ὦ ἀνόητοι oh **fools!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 572; ὦνόητε [prev. author] “V.” 252}}; opposed to προνοητικός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.9}}: _comparative_ **-ότερος**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Peregr.” 33}}; τὸ ἀ., opposed to τὸ νοῦν ἔχον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 30b; τῷ θνητῷ καὶ ἀ. [prev. author] “Phd.” 80b; τὸ ἀ. [τῆς ψυχῆς] [prev. author] “R.” 605b, etc.:—of animals, τὸ τῶν προβάτων ἦθος εὔηθες καὶ ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 610b23, compare 622a3.}}
**II.b** with _genitive_, **not understanding,** θεοῦ{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Maximus Tyrius Sophista 41.5; τῆς φωνῆς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 44, cf. Ecphantus Pythagoreus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.64.}}
**II.2** of acts, thoughts, etc., ἀ. γνῶμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 162 (Lyric poetry); δόξαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 12d; εὐχειρίη 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 35; ἀ. καὶ κενόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 530}}; οἴνου.. καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἀνοήτων and all other **follies,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Nu.” 417.}}
**II.2.b** **without mind,** ἀνόητα καὶ ἄνευ ζωῆς{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.5.1.}}
**III** _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 518, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 336e, etc.; ἀ. διακεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.4}}: _superlative_ **-ότατα**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 44.35}}:—also **ἀνο-ητεί**, {{Refs|“Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 1327, “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 2.313.}}

455:
Included with: ** ἀνοίγ-νῡμι**
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.10}}; ἀνοίγω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.88, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.37, 117}}, and _Attic dialect_ as {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 1.32 (συν-), +others: later ἀνοιγνύω 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 122, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.41.4}}: _imperfect_ ἀνἔῳγον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.221, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187, etc.; also ἀνῷγον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.168; rarely ἤνοιγον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.2 and 6.21}}; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ἀναοίγεσκον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.455; late ἀνεῴγνυον 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 4.81, etc.}}: _future_ ἀνοίξω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 179}}: _aorist_ ἀνέῳξα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “V.” 768, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.2, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.56}}_participle_ ἀνεῴξας {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” (add.) (from Antiphellus)}}; also ἤνοιξα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.13}} and in late Prose; _Ionic dialect_ ἄνοιξα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68 (best codices ἀνῷξα), 4.143, 9.118}}; _poetry_ ἀνῷξα{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.15, κἀνῷξε 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.103}} M.: _perfect_ ἀνέῳχα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.30, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 229; ἀνέῳγα 5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 2.22 (see. below)}}: _pluperfect_ ἀνεῴγει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 86(Pors.)}}:—_passive_, ἀνοίγνῠμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 923, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1326}}: late _future_ ἀνοιχθήσομαι{{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Isa.60.11, 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 33.13 (variant); ἀνοιγήσομαι LXX.Neh.7.3, “PMag.Par.” 1.358; ἀνεῴξομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.14}}: _perfect_ ἀνέῳγμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 56, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.4, etc.; ἀνῷγμαι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.47}}; later ἤνοιγμαι (δι-) best reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.80, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 2.9}}; _pluperfect_ ἀνέῳκτο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.14}}(_perfect_ {{Refs|2 ἀνέῳγα}} is used in _passive_ sense in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 4.39, “Cord.” 7}}, and later Prose, as {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.693d, NT.John.1.51, NT.2Cor.6.11, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nav.” 4}} (though he condemns it {{Refs|“Sol.” 8)}}; but in _Attic dialect_, only {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator “Fragmenta - Orat.” _Attic dialect_ 81)}}: _aorist_ ἀνεῴχθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1563}}, _subjunctive_ ἀνοιχθῆ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.37, _optative_ ἀνοιχθείην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59d}}, _participle_ ἀνοιχθείς{{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.130, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 216d; later ἠνοίχθην 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.35.7, LXX.Psa.105(106).17}}; and _aorist 2_ ἠνοίγην{{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Mark.7.35, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 14, etc.—In late Gr., very irregular forms occur, ἠνέῳξα LXX.Gen.8.6; ἠνέωχα “PMag.Par.” 1.2261; ἠνέῳγμαι NT.Rev.10.8, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 9.9; ἠνεῴχθην LXX.Gen.7.11}}; also _aorist 1_ _infinitive_ ἀνωίξαι{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 12.331; ἀνωίχθην 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 7.317}}:—**open,** of doors, etc., ἀναοίγεσκον μεγάλην κληῗδα **they tried to put back** the bolt **so as to open** [the door], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.455, compare 14.168; πύλας ἀνοῖξαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 604; θύραν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 768; also without θύραν, ἐπειδὴ αὐτῷ ἀνέῳξέ τις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310b, compare 314d}}; χηλοῦ δ᾽ ἀπὸ πῶμ᾽ ἀνέῳγε **took off** the cover **and opened** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.221; φωριαμῶν ἐπιθήματα κάλ᾽ ἀνέῳγεν 24.228}}; so ἀ. σορόν, θήκας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68, 187; κιβωτόν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.10}}; ἀ. σήμαντρα, σημεῖα, διαθήκην, **open** seals, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 6.4, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.30, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 68; and _metaphorically_, καθαρὰν ἀνοίξαντι κλῇδα φρενῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 660}}; ἀ. βίβλινον (i.e. οἶνον) **tap** it, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.15}}; γῆρυν ἀνοίξας, for στόμα, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 477}}; ἀ. φιλήματα kiss **with open mouths,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 2.37.}}
**b** **throw open** for use, γυμνάσιον{{Refs|“OGI” 529.11}}; κἀνῷξε σχολὰς **opened** school, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.103M.}}; εἰ ἀνοίξω ἐργαστήριο; shall I **open** a shop? {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Astrampsychus Onirocriticus “Oracula” 43p.5H.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **lay open, unfold, disclose,** ὄνομα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 322; ἔργ᾽ ἀναιδῆ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 515, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 326; λανθάνουσαν ἀτυχίαν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 674.}}
**3** as nautical term, _absolutely_, **get into the open sea, get clear of land,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.2, 5.13, 6.21; but ἁλὸς κέλευθον ἀ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.88}} is **to open** or **first show** the way over the sea.
**II** _passive_, **to be open, stand open, lie open,** ὄπισθε τῆς ἀνοιγομένης θύρης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9; ἀνεῳγμένην καταλαμβάνειν τὴν θύραν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174e; ἀνεῳγμένας πύλας Ἅιδου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 56; δικαστήρια ἀνοίγεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 405a; παρέξει τἀμπόρι᾽ ἀνεῳγμένα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1523; ἀνέῳκται τὸ δεσμωτήριον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.208; λέων τὰ ἐντὸς ἀνοιχθείς}}**cut open,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 497b17}}; κόλποι δι᾽ ἀλλήλων ἀνοιγόμενοι **opening** one into another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 4: _metaphorically_, θησαυρὸς ὡς ἀνοίγνυται κακῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 923.}}

456:
** ἀνοικο-δομέω**,
**build up,** τὰ χείλεα τοῦ ποταμοῦ.. ἀνοικοδόμηδε πλίνθοισι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.186.}}
**2** **wall up,** λαύρας καιναῖς πλίνθοισιν ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 100; θύραν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 128}}; πύλας uncertain reading in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.21 (in this sense ἀποικ- is a frequently variant).}}
**II** **build again, rebuild,** πόλιν καὶ τείχη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.89, cf. Jusj. cited in 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 81, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.4.19, etc.; ἀ. χώραν}}**occupy again with buildings,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.66}}:—_passive_, _metaphorically_, **to be exalted,** {{LXX|LXX.Mal.3.15.}}

458:
** ἀνομ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **lawlessness, lawless conduct,** opposed to δικαιοσύνη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96, 97; ἀ. νόμων κρατεῖ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1095 (Lyric poetry); ἀ. ἀμύνειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.7; ἀ. ὀφλισκάνειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 443; ἀντὶ αὐτονομίας.. εἰς ἀνομίας ἐμπίπτειν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.64, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.755b; ζῆν ἐν πάσῃ ἀναρχίᾳ καὶ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 575a.}}
**2** **the negation of law,** opposed to νόμος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.152.}}

459:
** ἄνομος**, ον,
**lawless, impious,** τράπεζα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.162}}; of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 142, +others; στρατός “Tr.” 1096; Ἐχίονος γόνος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 995}}; of things, θυσία{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 151}}; πάθη E {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Or.” 1455; μοναρχία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 302e: τὰ ἄνομα}}**lawless acts,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8}}: _comparative_ **-ώτερος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 285a}}. _adverb_ **-μως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1000, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.2, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.92.}}
**2** with _genitive_, ἀ. θεοῦ, i.e. **without (the Mosaic) Law** and therefore without God, {{NT|NT.1Cor.9.21.}}_adverb_ ἀνόμως, ={χωρὶς νόμου}, {{NT|NT.Rom.2.12.}}
**3** **illegal,** κατοχή{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 237 vii 11 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** (νόμος{{Refs|11)}} **unmusical,** νόμος ἄ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1142 (Lyric poetry).}}

460:
Included with: ** ἄνομος**, ον,
**lawless, impious,** τράπεζα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.162}}; of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 142, +others; στρατός “Tr.” 1096; Ἐχίονος γόνος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 995}}; of things, θυσία{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 151}}; πάθη E {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Or.” 1455; μοναρχία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 302e: τὰ ἄνομα}}**lawless acts,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8}}: _comparative_ **-ώτερος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 285a}}. _adverb_ **-μως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1000, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.2, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.92.}}
**2** with _genitive_, ἀ. θεοῦ, i.e. **without (the Mosaic) Law** and therefore without God, {{NT|NT.1Cor.9.21.}}_adverb_ ἀνόμως, ={χωρὶς νόμου}, {{NT|NT.Rom.2.12.}}
**3** **illegal,** κατοχή{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 237 vii 11 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** (νόμος{{Refs|11)}} **unmusical,** νόμος ἄ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1142 (Lyric poetry).}}

461:
** ἀνορθόω**,
_aorist_ ἀνώρθωσα E {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Alcaeus1138, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.64, etc.:}}_pluperfect_ with double augment ἠνωρθώκει variant in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 1039: the double augment is common in the _compound_ ἐπανορθόω}}:— **set up again, restore, rebuild,** τὸν νηόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.19; τὸ τεῖχος 7.208; τὸ στρατόπεδον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, etc.; τὸ σῶμά τινος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 364:}} —_middle_, ἀνορθοῦσθαι τὰ πίπτοντα τῶν οἰκοδομημάτων **have** them **rebuilt,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1322b20.}}
**2** **restore to health** or **well being.** πόλιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 46: τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 919d.}}
**3** **set straight again, set right, correct,** τινά{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1228; τὰ ἀλλότρια κακά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 346e.}}

462:
** ἀνόσιος**, ον,
more rarely α, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1316 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.157 (uncertain)}}, and later:— **unholy, profane,** opposed to ἄδικος, as ὅσιος to δίκαιος (see. ὅσιος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.1), of persons, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 611, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 353, etc.; ἀ. ὁ θεομισής 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 7a; ἄδικος καὶ ἀ. [prev. author] “Grg.” 505b.}}
**2** of things, ἔργον, μόρος, στόμα, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.114, 3.65, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 981, etc.; αὐδῶν ἀνόσι᾽ οὐδὲ ῥητά μοι [prev. author] “OT” 1289; ἀνόσια πάσχειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.7; ἀσεβὲς μηδὲν μηδὲ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.22; οὐ μόνον ἄνομον ἀλλὰ καὶ ἀ. [prev. author] “Lac.” 8.5}}; ἀ. νέκυς a corpse **with all the rites unpaid,** {{Refs|S “Ant.” 1071}}; ἀ. τι γεγένηται ἐμοῦ παρόντος the holy rites have been **profaned,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.84.}}
**II** _adverb_ **-ίως** **in unholy wise,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 257}}; κάτω γῆς ἀ. οἰκῶν **without funeral rites,** or **through an unholy deed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 677.}}

463:
** ἀνοχή**, ἡ,
**holding back, stopping,** especially of hostilities: hence mostly in _plural_, **armistice, truce,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.17}}; ἀνοχὰς ποιεῖσθαι Canon Laws texts cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.164; διδόναι 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.68; σπείσασθαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 29; αἱ Καλλισθένους ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.31; αἱ ἑξαετεῖς ἀ. 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.59}}; compare ἀνοκωχή.
**2** **time, opportunity,** οὐκ ἔδωκεν αὐτοῖς ἀνοχὴν ἐμβατεῦσαι{{LXX|LXX.1Mac.12.25}}; ἡμερῶν ἀ. **delay** of some days, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1068.15 (3rd c.AD).}}
**3** _plural_, ἀνοχαί, = Latin _feriae,_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 39.30.}}
**4** ἀνοχαὶδικῶν, = Latin _iustitium,_ {{Refs|[prev. author] 55.26.}}
**II** (ἀνέχομαι) **long-suffering, forbearance,** {{NT|NT.Rom.2.4, 3.26.}}
**II.2** ἀνοχὴν ἀναπαύλης διδόναι **permission** to rest, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.6.10.}}
**II.3** **relief from** disease, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “Synopsis” 8.3.4.}}
**III** ={ἀνατολή}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.157, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

464:
** ἀντᾰγων-ίζομαι**,
**I** **struggle against, prove a match for,** τινί, especially in war, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.109, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.72, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.8, etc.; ἀ. ταῖς παρασκεναῖς τινός 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.81; πρὸς τοὺς βαρβάρους “Inscription Prien.” 17.15, cf. NT.Heb.12.4.}}
**I.2** generally, **struggle, vie with,** τινί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38; περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.2; οἱ ἀνταγωνιζόμενοί τι}}**the parties in a lawsuit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.27; [οἱ ἐλευθέριοι] οὐκ ἀ. περὶ τῶν χρημάτων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1366b8.}}
**I.3** **act a part in rivalry with,** τινί{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 29.}}
**II** as _passive_, **to be set against,** τινί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 10.12.}}

467:
** ἀνταπο-δίδωμι**,
**give back, repay, tender in repayment** or **requital,** {{Refs|Batrachomyomachia 186}}; ἀ. τὸ ὅμοιον, τὸἴσον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.18, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.43; χάριν 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 2D.; τὴν ἴσην 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1379b7; ἀ. τροφεῖα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.49; ἀρετήν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.19; ὕβριν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.825c}}; τὰ αὐτὰ ἀ. **react** in the same way, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 79e}}; of counter-arguments, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prm.” 128d}}: _absolutely_, **pay back,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1367a21.}}
**2** **take vengeance,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Deu.32.35, +others, NT.Rom.12.19.}}
**II** **assign as a balance,** ἐναντίαν γένεσιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 71e.}}
**II.2** **make convertible,** τὴν μεταφορὰν τὴν ἐκ τοῦ ἀνάλογον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1407a15}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 79.}}
**II.2.b** Grammars, **make to correspond,** of correlatives (e.g. τοιοῦτος, οἷος), in _passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 254.19, “Synt.” 54.5, +others}}; so of μέν.. δέ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1407a23, 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 53, compare 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.4, +others}}
**II.3** _intransitive_, **answer to, correspond with,** εἰ μὴ ἀνταποδιδοίη τὰ ἕτερα τοῖς ἑτέροις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 72a}}, compare b; οὐκ ἀνταποδίδωσι τὸ ὅμοιον **there is** no similar **correspondent,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 347b32, compare “IA” 707b16, Pseudo-Alexander Rhetor Aphr. inSE 192.14.}}
**II.4** **give back words, exchange** 'tu quoque's', {{Refs|Pl “Phdr.” 236c.}}
**III** **deliver in turn,** τὸ σύνθημα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.58 (_passive_)}}; **explain in turn,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 87c.}}
**IV** **give back** a sound, of an echo, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 19}}; of troops, κραυγὴν ἀ.{{Refs|[prev. author] “Tim.” 27.}}

469:
** ἀνταπό-δοσις**, εως, ἡ,
**giving back in turn,** opposed to ἀποδοχή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.81}}; **rendering, requiting, repayment,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1133a3, 1163a11, +others; χάριτος 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 330, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.100; τῶν εὐεργεσιῶν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 14}}; **retribution,** {{LXX|LXX.Isa.61.2, 63.4, +others}}; **reprisals,** γίγνεται ἀ. ἔκ τινος{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.30.6}}; **reward,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.18(19).11, NT.Col.3.24.}}
**II** **turning back, opposite direction** or **course,** of a current, ἀ. ποιεῖσθαι{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.43.5, etc., compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.136b.}}
**II.2** **responsive sound,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 803a31.}}
**III** **alternation,** e.g. of action and reaction, περιόδων πρὸς ἀλλήλας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 1.12}}; **reaction,** probably in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.48U., compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Ventis” 10.}}
**III.2** Rhet., **parallelism** or **opposition of clauses** in a periodic sentence, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 23, compare 250}}; in a simile, **correspondence with** the object of comparison, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Quintilianus Rhetor Latinus “Inst.” 8.3.77.}}
**III.2.b** Grammars, **correlativity** of words such as τοιοῦτος, οἷος, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 54.1.}}
**III.2.c** **answering clause,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.11, 2.1; see l. in 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 20.6.}}
**III.3** **capping verses,** as a subject of competition, {{Refs|“Michel” 913 (from Teos).}}

470:
** ἀνταπο-κρίνομαι** [ῑ],
_middle_, **answer again,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Job.16.8, NT.Luke.14.6}}; **argue against,** τινί{{NT|NT.Rom.9.20.}}
**II** **correspond to,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 1.8.10, 11.}}

471:
** ἀντεῖπον**,
_aorist 2_ without any _present_ (compare ἀντερῶ, ἀντιλέγω, ἀνταγορεύω), **speak against** or **in answer, gainsay,** with _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 999, etc.; ἀ. τινὶ δεομένῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.136: _absolutely_, οὐδὲν ἀντειπεῖν ἔχω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 51}}; ἀ. πρός τινα or τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.61, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.3.3}}; **oppose,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 131a}}; ἀ. ὑπέρ τινος **speak** in one's defence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 545: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἀ. ἔπος}} **utter** a word **of contradiction,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1391}}; δύο λόγω περὶ τῶν αὐτῶν ἀντειπεῖν **speak on both sides of** a question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.1; τούτῳ ἂν δίκαιον λόγον ἀντείποιμι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28b.}}
**2** κακῶς ἀ. τινά **malign** him **in turn,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1053.}}

472:
** ἀντέχω**
or ἀντίσχω, _future_ ἀνθέζω; _participle_ ἀντισχήσων{{Refs 4th c.AD+|(in sense 11) 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 33.2:}}_aorist_ ἀντέσχον:— **hold against,** with _accusative_ et _genitive_, χεῖρ᾽ ἀ. κρατός **hold** one's hand **against** one's head so as to shade the eyes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1651: with _dative_, ὄμμασι δ᾽ ἀντίσχοις (-έχοις codices) τάνδ᾽ αἴγλαν}} **may'st** thou **keep** this sunlight **upon** his eyes, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 830 (Lyric poetry); τοὺς χαλινοὺς τῶν ἵππων 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 5.6.7.}}
**II** with _dative_, **hold out against, withstand,** Ἀρπάγῳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.175, compare 8.68. ; τοῖς δικαίοις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 78; τῇ ταλαιπωρίᾳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49; πρός τινα [prev. author] 6.22; πρὸς τοὺς καμάτους 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.6.10, etc.}}: with _accusative_, **endure,** ἀντέχομεν καμάτους{{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.299 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus); but in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.63 ἀ. τὰ τοῦ πολέμου}} rather belongs to the next _signification_, **hold out** as regards the war; so πολλὰἀ. {{Refs|[prev. work] 86.}}
**II.2** **hold out, endure,** with _participle_, ἡ Ἄζωτος.. ἐπὶ πλεῖστον χρόνον πολιορκουμένη ἀντέσχε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.157, compare 5.115, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.70; μηκέτι ἀντέχωσι τῷ πόνῳ διϊστάμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 81d}}; πολλάκις γιγνομένην ψυχὴν ἀντέχειν **last** through several states of existence, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 88a.}}
**II.3** _absolutely_, **hold out, stand one's ground,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.16, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 413, etc.; πῶς δύσμορος ἀντέχε; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 176 (Lyric poetry); νόσημα ἀντίσχει τὸν αἰῶνα πάντα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 11; ἔστ᾽ ἂν αἰὼν ἀντέχῃ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 337; βραχὺν χρόνον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.10}}; ἀ. ἐπὶ πολύ, ἐπί πλέον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.7, 65}}; ἀ. ἐλπίσιν in hope, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.26; ἀ. περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.16: peculiarly, ἀ. μὴ ὑπακοῦσαι}} I **hold out against.., refuse..,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.708a.}}
**II.3.b** of the rivers drunk by the Persian army, **hold out, suffice,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.196, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 413 (in full ἀ. ῥέεθρον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.58}}; ἀ. ὕδωρ παρέχων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 108); so ἀντέχει ὁ σῖτος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.65.}}
**II.4** **extend, reach,** ἐς ὅσον ἡ ἐπιστήμη ἀ.{{Refs|[prev. author] 6.69}}; **prevail,** διὰ τὴν λῃστείαν ἐπὶ πολὺ ἀντίσχουσαν{{Refs|1.7.}}
**III** _middle_, **hold before one against** something, with _accusative_ et _genitive_, ἀντίσχεσθε τραπέζας ἰῶν **hold out** the tables **against** the arrows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.74.}}
**III.2** with _genitive_ only, **hold on by, cling to,** ἐκείνου τῆς χειρός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. ; πέπλων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 750, compare “Ion” 1404; τῶν θυρῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 161: _metaphorically_, ἀ. τῶν ὄχθων}} **cling to** the banks, **keep close to** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.56}}; ἀ. Ἡρακλέος **cleave to** Hercules, i.e. **worship** him **above all,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.33}}; ἀ. τῆς ἀρετῆς, Latin _adhaerere virtuti,_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.134; ἀ. τοῦ πολέμου [prev. author] 7.53; τοῦ κέρδους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 354; τῆς θαλάσσης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.13; σωτηρίας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 33.6; τῆς ἀληθείας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 58e, compare “R.” 600d, +others; τῶν παραδεδομένων μύθων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1451b24}}; τῆς ἐλευθερίας Canon Laws texts cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.185; τῶν δικαίων “POxy.” 1203.30 (1st c.AD)}}. b. with _genitive_ _person_, **care for, support, 1Ep. Thess.**{{Refs|5.14.}}
**III.3** _absolutely_, αὐτὸς ἀντέχου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 893, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1121.}}
**III.4** with _double_ _genitive_ _person_ et _of things_, ἀνθέξεταί σου τῶν πατρῴων χρημάτων **will lay claim to** the property **from** you, **dispute** it **with** you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1658.}}
**III.5** **resist,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 574b; φονεῦσαι τοὺς ἀντεχομένους 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.49.}}
**III.6** **adhere,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 583a18: in Medicine texts, of constipation, γαστὴρ ἀντίσχετο 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.20; γαστρὸς ἀντεχομένης [prev. work] 17.}}

473:
** ἀντί**,
_preposition_ governing _genitive_:—originally sense, **over against.** (Cf. Sanskrit _ ánti_ 'opposite', 'facing', Latin _ante,_ etc.)
**A** USAGE:
**A.I** of Place, **opposite, over against,** formerly quoted from several places of {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, as 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.481 ἀντὶ ἐμεῖο (where now ἀντί᾽ ἐμεῖο, i.e. ἀντία) Τρώων ἄνθ᾽ ἑκατόν (i.e. ἄντα) 8.233}}; so ἄντ᾽ Αἴαντος (i.e. ἄντα){{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.415, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.115, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 727}}; but ἀντί is so used in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.6, “IG” 2.835}}**c-l**68; αἱ ὀπαὶ αἱ γιγνόμεναι ἀ. τόρμων mortises **facing** tenons, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 97.5}}; ἀντὶ μαιτύρων **in the presence of** witnesses, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Leg.Gort.” 1.40; ἀντὶ τῆς ὄψεως ἡμῶν 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus “Ars astronomica” 18.}}
**A.II** of Time, ἀντὶ νυκτός **the same** night, {{Refs|“SIG” 1025.43 (from Cos)}}; ἀντὶ ϝέτεος{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“GDI” 2561 A 45 (Delph.); ἀντ᾽ ἐνιαυτοῦ “IG” 5(2).266.8 (Mantinea, 1st c.BC)}}; ἀνθ᾽ ἡμέρας· δι᾽ ὅλης τῆς ἡμέρας, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; compare ἀντετοῦς.
**A.III** **instead, in the place of,** Ἕκτορος ἀντὶ πεφάσθαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.254; ἀντὶ γάμοιο τάφον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.307; so later πολέμιος ἀντὶ φίλου καταστῆναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87; ἀντὶ ἡμέρης νὺξ ἐγένετο [prev. author] 7.37; ἀντὶ φωτῶν σποδός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 434; τὸν πόλεμον ἀντ᾽ εἰρήνης μεταλαμβάνειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120, compare 4.20, 7.75; βασιλεύειν ἀντί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.4; also ἀντὶ ἄρχεσθαι ὑπ᾽ ἄλλων ἄρχειν ἁπάντων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.210, compare 6.32, 7.170 (where the usual _construction_ would be ἀντὶ τοῦ ἄρχεσθαι,compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.28, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.19, etc.); ὀργίλοι ἀντὶ θυμοειδοῦς γεγένηνται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 411c}}: sometimes used elliptically, ἦ 'τολμήσατ᾽ ἀντ᾽ ἐμοῦ δοῦναί τιν; i.e. ἀντὶ τοῦ ἐμοὶ δοῦναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 369, compare “OC” 448, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 58.}}
**A.III.2** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus often to denote equivalence, ἀντί νυ πολλῶν λαῶν ἐστιν ἀνήρ}} he is **as good as** many men, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.116}}; ἀντὶ κασιγνήτου ξεῖνος.. τέτυκται a guest is **as much as** a brother, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.546}}; ἀντί τοί εἰμ᾽ ἱκέταο I am **as** a suppliant, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.75, compare 8.163}}; so later τοῦτό σφι ἀντὶ λουτροῦ ἐστί serves **as** a bath, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.75}}; ὑπάρχειν ἀντὶ τῶν ἔνδον to be **as hostages for..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.5}}; δουλεύειν ἀντὶ ἀργυρωνήτων **just like** bought slaves, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 17.3; ἀντὶ [πλεύμονος] βράγχια 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 669a4.}}
**A.III.3** to denote exchange, **at the price of, in return for,** σοὶ δὲ θεοὶ τῶνδ᾽ ἀντὶ χάριν.. δοῖεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.650}}; νῆσον ἀντὶ χρημάτων παρέλαβον **for** money paid, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.59; ἀντ᾽ ἀργυρίου ἀλλάξασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 371d; ἀμείβειν τι ἀντί τινος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.17, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 646, 651; ἀντὶ ποίας εὐεργεσία; 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.40, etc.; τί δ᾽ ἐστὶν ἀνθ᾽ οὗ..; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 237; ὄνειδος ἀνθ᾽ ὅτου [prev. author] “OC” 967}}; δοίην ἀντ᾽ ἀνιῶν ἀνίας grief **for** grief, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 344; ἀντ᾽ ἀγαθῶν ἀγαθοῖσι βρύοις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 966}}:—hence ἀνθ᾽ ὧν **wherefore,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 31, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 264, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.83, NT.Luke.12.3}}; ἀντὶ τούτου **therefore,** {{NT|NT.Eph.5.31}}; but ἀνθ᾽ ὧν also for ἀντὶ τούτων ὄτι.., **because,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1068, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 434}}; ἀντὶ το; **wherefore? why?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1021}}; also ἀνθ᾽ ὧν ὅτι ἦτε.. **instead of being as you were..,** {{LXX|LXX.Deu.28.62.}}
**A.III.4** **for the sake of,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 237a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1110a21}}; with Verbs of entreaty, like{πρός} with _genitive_, ἀντὶ παίδων ἱκετεύομέν σε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1326.}}
**A.III.5** to mark comparison, ἓν ἀνθ᾽ ἑνός one **set against** the other, **compared with** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331b, “Lg.” 705b}}; χάριν ἀντὶ χάριτος, i.e. ever-increasing grace, {{NT|NT.John.1.16}}; **in preference to,** ἀφνεὸν βούλεται ἀντ᾽ ἀγαθοῦ{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 188; ἀντὶ αὐλοῦ καὶ ἀντὶ κιθάρας ὁ ἦχος ἀκούεται 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 71; αἱρεῖσθαί τι ἀντί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.3, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.1, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 9.1}}: even after Comparatives, πλέον ἀντὶ σοῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 577; μείζον᾽ ὅστις ἀντὶ τῆς αὑτοῦ πάτρας φίλον νομίζει [prev. author] “Ant.” 182}}; so (especially after a _negative_) ἄλλος ἀντ᾽ ἐμοῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 467, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 444, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 653; δόξαν ἀντὶ τοῦ ζῆν ἠγαπηκώς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 42.}}
**B** POSITION: ἀντί rarely follows its case, as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.650, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1277, “Ig” 5(1).1119 (Geronthrae, 4th c.BC), “Anthologia Graeca” 7.715 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus)}}; but the Grammars hold that it never suffers anastrophe.
**C** IN COMPOSITION (joined with other words), it signified,
**C.1** **over against, opposite,** as ἀντιβαίνω, ἀντίπορος.
**C.2** **against, in opposition to,** as ἀντιλέγω, ἀντίβιος.
**C.3** **one against another, mutually,** as ἀντιδεξιόομαι.
**C.4** **in return,** as ἀντιβοηθέω.
**C.5** **instead of,** as ἀντιβασιλεύς, ἀνθύπατος.
**C.6** **equal to, like,** as ἀντίθεος, ἀντίπαις, ἀντίδουλος.
**C.7** **corresponding, counter,** as ἀντίφορτος, ἀντίτυπος.

474:
** ἀντιβάλλω**,
**throw against** or **in turn,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.25 (the _accusative_ _person_ being understood); βέλος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.22.4: with _dative_, ἀ. ἀκοντίοις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 25}}; ἀ. τῷ κωρύκῳ **practise by striking against** the sack, in the gymnasium, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Legal.” 5}}; **put back** a protruding bone, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus}}**in Hp.Fract.**{{Refs|12.285C.}}
**II** **put one against the other, compare, collate,** of Mss., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.1.54, 17.1.5, “POxy.” 1479 (1st c.BC, _passive_)}}; **match, compare with,** λέοντι τίς αἰετὸν ἀντιβάλοιτ;{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.68}}; λόγους ἀ. πρὸς ἀλλήλους **exchange** words in conversation, {{NT|NT.Luke.24.17}}; πρὸς ἑαυτὸν ἀ. τὸ γεγονός **weigh** with oneself, {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.11.13.}}
**III** in _middle_, **change,** μορφήν{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|uncertain 1. 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.16.}}

477:
** ἀντί-θεσις**, εως, ἡ,
**opposition,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 257e, 258b; ἀντίθεσιν ἔχειν πρός τι}}**correspond to..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 503a25}}; **resistance,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 12.200 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus).}}
**2** in Logic, **opposition** of propositions, in _plural_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Interpretatione” 19b20, “Top.” 113b15, “Metaphorical” 1054a23.}}
**2.b** **substitution of the contradictory,** as 'not-man' for 'man', ἡ σὺν -θέσει ἀντιστροφή, conversion **by negation,** e.g. 'man is an animal ∴ what is not an animal cannot be a man', {{Refs|Anonymus “in SE” 15.23, +others}}
**3** Rhet., **antithesis,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.2, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1410a22}}; in forensic oratory, **counter-proposition,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.4, +others}}
**4** Grammars, **change** or **transposition** of a letter,{{Refs|Hdn.Gr. 2.945, Diomedes Grammaticus 1.442K.}}

478:
** ἀντικαθίστημι**,
_Ionic dialect_ ἀντικατ-, _future_ -καταστήσω:— **replace, substitute,** ἄλλα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.93}}; μὴ ἐλάσσω ἀντικαταστῆσαι πάλιν **replace** an equal quantity of gold, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13}}; ἄλλους ἀ. **set up** others **in their stead,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 838a3.}}
**2** **set against, oppose,** τινὰ πρός τινα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.93}}; **establish as a counterpart,** τινά τινι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 591a.}}
**3** **set up** or **bring back again,** ἀ. ἐπὶ τὸ θαρρεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65}}; **rally,** τοὺς θορυβηθέντας{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.11.}}
**II** _passive_, with _aorist 2_ and _perfect_ _active_; also _aorist 1_ κατεστάθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.38}}:—**to be put in another's place, reign in his stead,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37, X. [prev. cited]}}
**II.2** **to be pitted against** another, **opposed,** _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71, 3.47, etc.; τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 7.5.}}
**II.2.b** in lawsuits, **to be confronted with,** τινί, πρός τινα, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 97.9 (2nd c.AD), “BGU” 168.11(2nd c.AD).}}

480:
** ἀντίκειμαι**,
_3rd.pers.plural_ ἀντικέαται{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 2.2.4, used as}}_passive_ of ἀντιτίθημι:—**to be set over against, correspond with,** τιμὰ ἀγαθοῖσιν ἀ. **is held out to** them **as a fitting reward,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).26:}} —ἀντικείμενος, ὁ, name of a **bandage,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de fasciis - Opera Hippocratis et Galeni” 12.515C.}}
**II** **to be opposite to,** of places, τινός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aeër.” 4; τινί 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.5.15}}; of things, **to be opposite** or **opposed,** πρὸς ἄλληλα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 258b}}; ἀ. κατὰ διάμετρον in a circle, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 277a23, +others}} _adverb_ -μένως, συνέστηκεν{{Refs|“PA” 654a11.}}
**II.2** **to be opposed,** in various ways, {{Refs|“Cat.” 11b17, “Metaphorical” 1055a38, +others; in Logic, αἱ -κειμέναι προτάσεις “APr.” 63b24, +others}} _adverb_ ἀντικειμένως{{Refs|“Metaphorical” 1054b15, etc.}}; propositions are **opposed** either contradictorily (ἀντιφατικῶς) or contrarily (ἐναντίως), {{Refs|“Int.” 17b16}}; ἀντικείμενα defined as ὧν τὸ ἕτερον τοῦ ἑτέρου ἀποφάσει πλεονάζει{{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.70 compare 82, +others}}
**II.3** Rhet., ἀντικειμένη [λέξις] **antithetical,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1409b35; ἀντικειμένως εἰπεῖν [prev. work] 1401a5, compare 1410b29; ἀντικείμενα κῶλα 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 22.}}
**III** **resist, be adverse,** ἀντικείσομαι τοῖς ἀντικειμένοις σοι{{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.23.22, cf. LXX.Isa.66.6, +others, NT.Luke.13.17, +others}}; **to be hurtful,** τοῖς σώμασι{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Procopius Gazaeus Rhetor “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 27.}}

482:
** ἀντιλαμβάνω**,
**receive instead of,** χρυσοῦ δώματα πλήρη τᾶς ἥβας ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 646 (Lyric poetry)}}; mostly without a _genitive_, οὔτε κακοὺς εὖ δρῶν εὖ πάλιν ἀντιλάβοις **receive in turn,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 108; κἂν.. ᾖ σώφρων.. σώφρον᾽ ἀντιλήψεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 741; ἡδονὴν δόντας.. κακίαν.. ἀ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58; ἔρανον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 133o b40}}; ἀ. ἄλλην [χώραν] **seize in return, get instead,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.143; ἀ. ἄλλους τινάς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.12, compare 8.7.16; χάριτα “Anthologia Graeca” 6.191 (from Longus).}}
**II** mostly in _middle_, with _perfect_ _passive_ **-είλημμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.15, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 130e}}: with _genitive_, **lay hold of,** σαπροῦ πείσματος ἀντελάβου{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1362; ἄκρου τοῦ στύρακος ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 184a, compare “Prt.” 317d, +others; τῇ ἀριστερᾷ ἀ. τοῦ τρίβωνος [prev. work] 335d}}; φιλίου χωρίου ἀ. **gain** or **reach** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.77, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 242:}} _absolutely_, **-όμενος** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.22}}. b. _metaphorically_c._genitive_, **lay hold of,** τῆς σωτηρίας, τῆς ἐλευθερίας, τοῦ ἀσφαλοῦς, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.61, 62, 3.22}}; **lay claim to,** τοῦ θρόνου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 777, 787; τοῦ πατρικοῦ μέρους “BGU” 648.10 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** **help, take part with, assist,** οὐκ ἀντιλήψεσθ;{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 464}}; of persons, ἀ. Ἑλλήρων **to take** their **part,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.13; ἀ. τῶν ἀσθενούντων NT.Act.20.35, etc.: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.70:— also in}}_passive_, ἀντειλημμένη **having received help**, {{Refs|“BGU” 1105.21 (Aug.), +others}}
**II.3** **take part** or **share in** a thing, **take in hand,** τῶν πραγμάτων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.6, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.20, etc.; τοῦ πολέμου 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.101; τῆς θαλάττης 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.39.14; τῆς Ἀφροδίτης 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 219.15; τῆς παιδείας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 534d}}; ἀ. τοῦ λόγου **seize on** the conversation (to the interruption of the rest), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 336b: _absolutely_, ἀρχόμενοι πάντες ὀξύτερον ἀ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.8, compare 8.106.}}
**II.4** **take hold of for the purpose of finding fault, reprehend, attack,** ἡμῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 239d, compare “R.” 497d, etc.}}; τοῦδε ἀντιλαβώμεθα **let** us **attack** the question, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 169d}}; ἀ. ὡς ἀδύνατον.. **to object** that.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 251b: _absolutely_, [prev. author] “Grg.” 506a.}}
**II.5** **take fast hold of,** i.e. **captivate,** ὁ λόγος ἀντιλαμβάνεταί μου{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Phd.” 88d, compare “Prm.” 130e, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 19.}}
**II.6** of plants, **take hold,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.1.5}}; of scions, **unite, CP**{{Refs|1.6.4.}}
**II.7** **grasp with the mind, perceive, apprehend,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 370a}}; noted as an obsol, word for συνίημι by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 7:—so of the senses, ἀ. κατὰ τὴν ἀκοήν, ὀσφρήσει, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.50, 64, cf. Phot. p.148R., 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Topicorum libros octo commentaria - pars” 103.1, +others}}
**III** in _middle_ also, **hold back,** ἵππου τῷ χαλινῷ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 10.15, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1188b6}}; **interrupt, Aud.** {{Refs|802b26.}}
**IV** _active_ in sense of _middle_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Problemata - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 1.38.}}

483:
** ἀντιλέγω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, Comedy texts, and _Attic dialect_ Prose (compare ἀνταγορεύω)}}: _future_ ἀντιλέξω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 993, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 998, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 8.10 (but the common _future_ is ἀντερῶ)}}: _aorist_ ἀντέλεξα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 409, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1040 (but the _aorist_ commonly used is ἀντεῖπον):}}_perfect_ ἀντείρηκα: _future_ _passive_ ἀντειρήσομαι:—**speak against, gainsay, contradict,** τινί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.30, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 216b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.6.13, etc.; περί τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.53, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.8}}; ὑπὲρ τῶν δικαίων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 3.5.12; πρός τι 1.2.17; πρός πάντα τὰ δίκαια 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 888:}} —often followed by a dependent clause, ἀ. ὡς.. **declare in opposition** or **answer** that.., χρησμοῖσι οὐκ ἔχω ἀντιλέγειν ὡς οὐκ εἰσὶ ἀληθέες{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.77, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 980, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.25, etc.; οὐ τοῦτό γ᾽ ἀντιλέγουσιν, ὡς οὐ.. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287b23; also ἀ. ὑπέρ τινος ὡς.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.45: so with}}_infinitive_, ἀ. ποιήσειν ταῦτα, ἤν.. **to reply** that they will.., if.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.28; ἀ. μὴ κτεῖναι Μυτιληναίους [prev. author] 3.41; ἀ. τὸ μὴ οὐ ἀξιοῦσθαί τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.20.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _of things_, ἴσ᾽ ἀντιλέξαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 409; ἀ. τὸν ἐναντίον λόγον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 8.11}}; μῦθον ἀ. τινί **tell** one tale **in reply to** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 806; ἀντιλέγομεν πρᾶγμά τι 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 8}}:—_passive_, **to be disputed, questioned,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.37}}; of a place, ὑπό τινος ἀντιλεγόμενον **counter-claimed,**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 3.2.30; ἀντιλέγεσθαι μικρὸν πρός τινα περί τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.15; τὰ ἀντιλεγόμενα}}**points in dispute,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.44}}; πρὸς τὰ ἀντειρημένα κτλ., title of work by Chrysippus, {{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.8}}; τόπος ἀντιλλεγόμενος (sic) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).443.15 (Megalopolis, 2nd c.BC): _absolutely_, -λέγεται περί τινος 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.6.6}}; of the genuineness of literary works, **to be disputed,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.839c.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **speak in opposition,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.42, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 993, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1076, etc.; ὁ ἀντιλέγων}}**the opponent,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335a; οἱ ἀντιλέγοντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.53; λαὸς ἀπειθῶν καὶ -λέγων LXX.Isa.65.2.}}

484:
** ἀντί-ληψις**,
later **ἀντί-λημψις**, εως, ἡ, **receiving in turn** or **exchange,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120}}; **counterclaim,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.5.}}
**II** (from _middle_) **laying hold of in turn, reciprocation,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 208}}; of cultivated plants, **giving a return,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.6.6}}; of a vine **laying hold** by its tendrils, {{Refs|[prev. work] 2.18.2.}}
**II.b** **taking in hand,** τοῦ λ[ε]ιτουργήματος {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy” 900.13 (4th c.AD).}}
**II.2** ={ἀντιλαβή}, **hold, support,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 5.7}}; of a bandage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 9; ἀντίληψιν βοηθείας ἔχειν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.30}}; ἀ. διδόναι τινί give one **a handle,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.966e; ἀ. παρέχειν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 2.}}
**II.3** **defence, succour**, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 42.38(2nd c.BC), “PAmh.” 35.58 (2nd c.BC), “BGU” 1187.27 (1st c.BC), LXX.Psa.21(22).20, +others, NT.1Cor.12.28, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 7.3.}}
**II.4** **claim to** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.5.}}
**II.5** **objection,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 87a, “Sph.” 241b, “Hp.Ma.” 287a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 18, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 1.1, +others}}: in forensic oratory, **plea of justification,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ τῶν στάσεων” 2, +others, 5th c.AD: Syrianus Philosophus et Rhetor “in Hermogenem commentaria” 2p.79R.}}; **discussion,** θεολογικὴ ἀ.{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 1.8.}}
**II.6** **grasping with the mind, apprehension,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 250, “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.206, 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 4; φυσικὴν -ψιν ποιεῖσθαί τινος 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.15}}; οὐκ ἐπιστρέφει τὴν ἀ. does not attract the **attention,** [{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor] “Rh.” p.190H.}}; of sensuous **perception,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Stoic. 2.230, 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 100b, Anonymus “in Tht.” 59.48, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1003, Alex Aphr. “in Top.” 91.5; ποιοτήτων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.625b, compare 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus 1.}}
**II.7** of disease, **seizure, attack,** τῶν ἀκρωτηρίων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49.}}

485:
** ἀντιλογ-ία**, ἡ,
**contradiction, controversy,** ἀ. χρησμῶν πέρι λέγειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.77}}; ἡμέας.. ἐς ἀ. παρέξομεν will offer ourselves to **argue the point,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.87}}; ἐδόκεον ἀντιλογίης κυρήσειν expected to be allowed **to argue it,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 88; εἰς -ίαν κατέστησαν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 75.1; -ίας ἅπτεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 454b; ἐς -ίαν τινί γενέσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.73; ἀ. καί λοιδορία 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.32}}; ἀντιλογίαν ἔχει it is open to **contradiction,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1418b25, compare 1414b3}}: in _plural_, **opposing arguments,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 775; δι᾽ ἀντιλογιῶν καταλλαγῆναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.59; ἀ. πρός τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.20; ἐς -ίαν ἐλθεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.31}}; ἀντιλογίαν ἐν αὑτῷ ἔχειν to have **grounds for defence** in itself, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.87}}; ἄνευ -ίας without **dispute**, {{Refs|“BGU” 1133.15 (Aug.),etc.}}
**2** later, **quarrel, dispute,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 2p.56(3rd c.BC), “PGrenf.” 1.38.8 (2nd-1st c.BC), NT.Heb.12.3, etc.}}
**3** **right, claim,** τοῦ αὐτοῦ λάκκου{{Refs 6th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1892 (6th c.AD).}}

487:
** ἀντίλυτρ-ον**, τό,
**ransom,** {{NT|NT.1Tim.2.6.}}
**2** **antidote, remedy,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 593.}}

488:
** ἀντιμετρ-έω**,
**measure out in turn, give** one thing **as compensation for** another, τί τινι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 19:}} —_passive_, ἀντιμετρηθήσεται ὑμῖν it **shall be measured in turn,** {{NT|NT.Matt.7.2, NT.Luke.6.38.}}
**II** Astrology texts, **correspond in ascension,** {{Refs|Cat.Codex Astr. 8(4).187.}}

492:
** ἀντιπαρ-έρχομαι**,
**pass by on the opposite side,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Luke.10.31: with _accusative_ loci, “Anthologia Graeca” 12.8 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus).}}
**II** **come up ana help,** as **against** an enemy,{{LXX|LXX.Wis.16.10.}}
**III** **enter in place of,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 29.}}
**IV** **penetrate,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.248.}}

495:
** ἀντιπέρ-ᾱν**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἀντιπέρ-ην**, _adverb_ ={ἀντιπέρᾱς}, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.9, compare 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.177, +others}}; also κατ᾽ ἀντιπέραν, with _genitive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.41.11.}}
**II** _adjective_, Ἀσίδα τ᾽ ἀντιπέρην τε Asia and **the opposite coast,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 2.9.}}

496:
** ἀντιπίπτω**,
**collide,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 915b18}}; **fall upon,** ταῖς σπείραις{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.19.5.}}
**2** **resist,** ἀντιπῖπτον **resisting body,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 961b3; ἀ. τινί NT.Act.7.51}}; μηδὲν ἀντιπεσόντα without **demur**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 36.21 (2nd c.BC)}}; τῆς φράσεως οὐκ -ούσης{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 123.5; εἰ μηδὲν -πίπτει “POxy.” 1473.20 (3rd c.AD),compare 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 16.73}}; ἀντιπῖπτον an **objection,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteorologicorum librum primum commentarium - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 58.3; ἡ τοῦ ἀντιπίπτοντος λύσις Aps. p.238H.}}
**3** of circumstances, **to be adverse,** τινί{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.2.1, etc.: _absolutely_, τῆς τύχης -ούσης [prev. work] 28.2}}; of contrary winds, {{Refs|4.44.9}}; **tell against, conflict with** (fact or theory), {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 8, +others}}
**II** **to fall in a contrary direction,** αἱ σκιαί{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.1.19.}}

498:
** ἀντιτάσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ ἀντιτάττω, **set opposite to, range in battle against,** τὸ ἄριστον ἀ. Πέρσῃσι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.110; τίν᾽ ἀντιτάξεις τῷδ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 395, compare 408, etc.}}; ἀ. τὸν νόμον πρὸς τὴν ἀναίδειαν **set** the law **in opposition to** their impudence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.61, etc.}}:—so in _middle_, πρὸς τὸ ἐμπειρότερον αὐτῶν τὸ τολμηρότερον ἀντιτάξασθε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.87; τῶν Ἑλλήνων τινὰ ἀρετὴν τῇ Ξέρξου δυνάμει ἀντιτάξασθαι [prev. author] 3.56.}}
**II** _middle_, **set oneself against, meet face to face,** ἀντιτάξομαι κτενῶν σε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 622, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, etc.; περί τῶν πρωτείων ἡμῖν ἀντιτάξασθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.27}}:—_passive_, **to be drawn out in array against,** τινί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.6; πρὸς τὸ διπλήσιον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.103; πόλιν -ομένην πρὸς πόλιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.18, etc.; κατά τινας [prev. author] “HG” 4.2.18; τὸ ἀντιτετάχθαι ἀλλήλοις τῇ γνώμῃ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.83: _absolutely_, uncertain in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1144.}}
**II.2** generally, **oppose, resist,** {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.25.8, NT.Act.18.6, NT.Rom.13.2, etc.}}
**II.3** **set against, compare,** {{LXX|LXX.Pro.3.15 (_passive_).}}

499:
** ἀντίτῠπ-ος**, ον,
rarely η, ον, see below 11.1a: (τύπτω): — **repelled** by a hard body, τύπος ἀ. **blow and counter-blow,** of the hammer and anvil, Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.67, compare 68}}; of sound, **echoed, echoing,** στόνος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 694(Lyric poetry), 1460(Lyric poetry), compare “APl.” 4.154 (2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista or 1st c.BC: Archias)}}; κατὰ τὸ ἀ. by **repercussion,** of an echo, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dom.” 3}}; of light, **reflected,** ἀκτῖνες{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 519, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 9.822.}}
**2** **corresponding,** as the stamp to the die, ἅγια ἀ. τῶν ἀληθινῶν **figuring** or **representing** the true, {{NT|NT.Heb.9.24, cf. NT.1Pet.3.21; ἀ. τοῖς δακρύοις χάριτα “IG” 14.1320}}; **resembling,** with _dative_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 26.327; μίμημα [prev. work] 8.23}}: hence, **feigned, counterfeit,** {{Refs|1.423, +others}}
**2.b** **corresponding,** φιλότης **mutual** affection, {{Refs|13.552.}}
**2.c** _substantive_ ἀντίτυπος, ὁ, or ἀντίτυπον, τό, **image,** Ἄμμωνος κεραοῦ χάλκεον ἀ.{{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 835 (from Berytus)}}; ἀντίτυπον, τό, ={ἀντίγραφον}, **reproduction, copy,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|POxy. 1470.6(4th c.AD)}}: _metaphorically_, **antitype,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.9.6.}}
**II** _active_, **repelling,** as a hard body does: hence,
**II.1** **firm, resistent,** χωρίον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 43}}; **rigid, inelastic,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 9.739 (4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator Aegypt.)}}; -ώτερα ὄντα, of a horse's fetlocks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.4; ἀντιτύπᾳ δ᾽ ἐπὶ γᾷ πέσε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 134}}; οἱ ἐν ἀντιτύποις περίπατοι walking on **hard ground,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 885a36}}; ἀντιτυπώτατον εἶδος, explanation of σκληρόν, **most resistent,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 62c.}}
**II.1.b** _metaphorically_, **stubborn, obstinate,** ἄνθρωποι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 156a; μάχη ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 6.2}}; **harsh-sounding,** ἁρμονίαι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 22, compare 16; ἀ. ἀκοῦσαι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 12.15}}; of colour, **glaring,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 22.}}
**II.2** **opposed to,** ἦθος δόλιον πίστιος ἀντίτυπον **the reverse of..,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1244}}; ἀ. Διός **the adversary** of Zeus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 521 (Lyric poetry)}}; **adverse,** of events, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.11: simply, ἀ. τινι}}**opposite, over against,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.31.8.}}
